// SignatureIndex — the WHOLE-PROGRAM DECLARATION index: every function, every ranged typealias and
// every top-level constant DECLARED in any file, read straight off the TOKENS before any body is
// parsed.
//
// WHY IT MUST EXIST, AND WHY IN THE PARSER. Three source-level decisions cannot be postponed past
// the parser, and each needs a declaration that may live in another file:
//
//   • A CALL'S RESULT TYPE. A bool `and`/`or` is CONTROL FLOW (`Parser.emitShortCircuit` — blocks
//     and a merge phi, which no later pass can retrofit); `not` picks a different OPCODE per
//     operand type; and every operand rule in TypeRules reads a value's tag. By the time a later
//     pass could supply a callee's return type, every decision that needed it has already been
//     made.
//
//   • A SHIFT'S FILL. `shr` is ARITHMETIC on a signed left operand and LOGICAL on an unsigned one
//     (TypeRules' ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE), and Maxon's unsigned type is a ranged alias whose low bound
//     is 0. The two are DIFFERENT x64 instructions, and the Std tier they lower into is TYPELESS —
//     an i64 there carries no signedness — so the opcode must be chosen while the declared type is
//     still in view, which is here. `u64.max shr 60` is 15 or -1 depending on it.
//
//   • A TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT'S VALUE. A `let` at module scope is a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT that every
//     use INLINES as a literal (`Parser.topLevelConstantReference`), so the parser needs the NUMBER,
//     not a promise of one. It cannot fold the initializer itself: a forward reference
//     (`let TOTAL = FIRST + SECOND` above `let FIRST = 30`) and a cross-file `export let` are both
//     ordinary, and neither is in view while one file is parsed. So the fold happens HERE, after
//     every file's declarations are known — see `evaluateInitializers`.
//
// So the RANGES travel beside the return types, and the CONSTANTS beside both. An alias declared in
// `a.maxon` and used in `b.maxon` has to reach `b`'s parse, or the shift in `b` is a silent wrong
// answer — and reading the file-local alias table instead would be the identical mistake this index
// exists to fix, wearing a different hat.
//
// The FILE-LOCAL version of this — one token sweep of the file being parsed — is why the
// single-file case was right. Cross-file it was not, and the failure was a WRONG ANSWER, not a
// rejection:
//
//     a.maxon:  export function isReady() returns bool
//     b.maxon:  let x = isReady()
//               return x + 41        ⇒ shv2 COMPILED it, and returned 42
//                                    ⇒ bootstrap: E2004: Cannot operate on bool and int
//
// The parser reported an out-of-file callee as `MaxonType.unresolved`; TypeRules maps that to
// `TypeClass.unknown`; and `unknown` AGREES WITH EVERYTHING. That deferral is the right move for a
// type you genuinely cannot see, and a catastrophic one for a type you simply did not LOOK for.
// **The deferral was never the defect. Not looking was.** So this index looks — at every file's
// tokens, before any file's parse — and `unresolved` is no longer an answer for a callee some file
// declares. (Its twin was worse: agreement satisfied by deferral let `(boolean, unresolved)` reach
// `wordOpResultTag`'s bool-first arm, so `flag and crossFileInt()` MINTED a merge phi tagged
// `boolean` carrying the int 7.)
//
// THE ID PROBLEM — why a signature travels by NAME. A `MaxonType.named(id)` is meaningless outside
// the `TypeNameInterner` that minted the id, and this index is built BEFORE, and APART FROM, the
// per-file artifact interners the parser fills (ParseStaging's parallel-ready discipline: a file's
// parse touches no shared state). So the index owns its own interner, and a parser ADOPTS a
// callee's return type out of it — the type's NAME is re-interned into the parser's own artifact
// table, so the `MaxonType` handed back is indistinguishable from one that file had parsed itself.
// `returnTypeOf` is the one door, and it is the one place the two id spaces meet.

// The shape discriminators the signature hash mixes — one per arm of the two type unions, so two
// declarations that differ only in shape (`returns bool` vs `returns int`) cannot hash alike.
//
// Small distinct constants, NOT the enums' ordinals: an ordinal is not a value in Maxon, and — the
// real reason — a hash that moved when an enum's arms were REORDERED would invalidate every parse
// memo in the project for a change that means nothing.
let ReturnShapeVoid = 1 as ContentHash
let ReturnShapeValue = 2 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeBoolean = 3 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeInteger = 4 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeFloat = 5 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeNamed = 6 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeExitCode = 7 as ContentHash
let TypeShapeUnresolved = 8 as ContentHash

// A ranged typealias's declaration, mixed into the same hash as a function's. Its own
// discriminator, so a file that replaces a function with an alias of the same name moves the hash
// rather than colliding two differently-shaped declarations on it.
let DeclShapeAlias = 9 as ContentHash

// A declared struct type's shape discriminator — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a `typealias` and starts being a `type` must move the hash, not collide on it.
let DeclShapeStruct = 14 as ContentHash

// A declared enum/union's shape discriminator — `DeclShapeStruct`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a `type` and starts being an `enum` must move the hash rather than collide.
let DeclShapeEnum = 20 as ContentHash

// Whether a declared type is a `union` (vs an `enum`), mixed into the hash beside its name. Two
// distinct constants because the only thing separating a payload-free union from an enum is the
// diagnostic WORDING, and a reader that meets a non-exhaustive match must re-parse — and so print
// "union" rather than "enum" — when `enum` becomes `union`.
let EnumKindEnum = 21 as ContentHash
let EnumKindUnion = 22 as ContentHash

// The `MaxonType.structRef` arm's discriminator. It is DISTINCT from `TypeShapeNamed`, and that is
// load-bearing rather than tidy: `named(Point)` and `structRef(Point)` carry the same NAME and mean
// different things in every reader — one is a ranged int, the other a pointer — so a hash that
// could not tell them apart would hand a reader a memo built under the wrong one.
let TypeShapeStructRef = 15 as ContentHash

// The `MaxonType.string` arm's discriminator (P1.2 wave A). Distinct like every other shape: a
// `String` return type hashes differently from an `int` one, so a memo built under one cannot be
// served for the other.
let TypeShapeString = 23 as ContentHash

// A FUNCTION value's type shape (P1.5-A1) — so a `returns UnaryOp` return type hashes distinctly. Its
// own discriminator for `TypeShapeStructRef`'s reason. (A function type reaches the SCANNED signature
// path only if a future producer stores one; the sweep records a function-alias reference as `named`
// until the alias is known, so today this arm is exhaustiveness insurance, hashed not panicked.)
let TypeShapeFunction = 29 as ContentHash

// A declared FIELD's mutability, mixed into the hash beside its name and type (P1.1a wave 2). The
// struct twin of `DeclShapeConstant`/`DeclShapeMutable`, and distinct constants rather than a reuse
// of them: those two discriminate a top-level `let` from a `var`, and colliding a field's `let` with
// a global's would let an edit that swaps one for the other land on the hash the other already
// occupied. What rides is ACCEPTANCE — `var count` → `let count` must turn every other file's
// `c.count = 5` into E2013, and a reader whose memo did not move would go on compiling it.
let FieldShapeMutable = 16 as ContentHash
let FieldShapeImmutable = 17 as ContentHash

// Whether a field declares a DEFAULT, mixed beside the payload when it does. Two constants and not
// one, because "has no default" must hash differently from "defaults to 0" — the payload alone
// cannot say so, and those two declarations accept different programs (`Self{}` is E3086 under the
// first and legal under the second).
let FieldShapeNoDefault = 18 as ContentHash
let FieldShapeDefaulted = 19 as ContentHash

// The two shapes a top-level constant's ANSWER hashes under: it evaluated to a value, or it did
// not. Their own discriminators for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason — a name that stops being a function
// and starts being a constant must move the hash rather than collide.
let ConstShapeValue = 10 as ContentHash
let ConstShapeNoValue = 11 as ContentHash

// A declaration's MUTABILITY, mixed into the hash beside its value. `let X = 5` and `var X = 5`
// are the same name and the same number and compile to COMPLETELY different code — one inlines
// as a literal, the other loads from `.data` — so a reader's parse memo must move when one
// becomes the other. Without these, changing `let` to `var` would leave every reader's memo
// valid and every reader still inlining a number that now has storage behind it.
let DeclShapeConstant = 12 as ContentHash
let DeclShapeMutable = 13 as ContentHash

// A function's PER-PARAMETER CONSUME BITS (P1.4a wave 2), mixed into the hash beside its name so a
// cross-file caller re-parses when a callee's consume set changes. What rides is ACCEPTANCE and OWNERSHIP:
// a param that becomes CONSUMED turns every caller's `f(ownedLocal)` into a move (the local is poisoned,
// E3102 on reuse) and its `f(borrowedValue)` into E2015, while the caller's own bytes never move — so a
// reader whose memo did not move would keep the old drop behaviour, a double-free or a leak. Its own
// discriminator for `DeclShapeAlias`'s reason. `ConsumeBitSet`/`ConsumeBitClear` distinguish a set bit
// from a clear one so "param 0 consumed, param 1 not" hashes differently from the reverse.
let DeclShapeParamConsume = 24 as ContentHash
let ConsumeBitSet = 25 as ContentHash
let ConsumeBitClear = 26 as ContentHash

// A function's `throws E` clause discriminator (P1.4b). Its own discriminator for `DeclShapeAlias`'s
// reason: a callee that GAINS or CHANGES a throws type changes whether each call site needs a `try`
// (E3055/E3057) and what type the `(e)` binding recovers, so a caller's parse memo must move when the
// callee's throws clause does. Only THROWING functions are recorded (absence = non-throwing default),
// exactly as `recordParamConsume` records only consuming ones — so a non-throwing function costs no
// hash movement.
let DeclShapeThrows = 27 as ContentHash

// A FUNCTION typealias's shape discriminator (P1.5-A1) — `DeclShapeAlias`'s twin, for its reason: a name
// that stops being a ranged alias and becomes a function one, or whose function return type changes,
// changes what a caller binding a value of it compiles to, so a caller's parse memo must move with it.
let DeclShapeFunctionAlias = 28 as ContentHash

// The separator between a file's path and a constant's bare name in a FILE-PRIVATE constant's
// storage key. `|` is legal in neither a Maxon identifier nor a Windows path, so the composite is
// unambiguous. Ported verbatim from v1's `fileScopedTopLevelKey` (Project.maxon:189-191), which
// solves the identical problem the identical way.
let FileScopedKeySeparator = "|"

// An index into the constant ARENA (`ProgramSignatures.decls`). Wide range: declaration counts
// are unbounded.
export typealias TopLevelDeclIndex = int(0 to u64.max)
export typealias TopLevelDeclIndexArray = Array with TopLevelDeclIndex
typealias TopLevelDeclIndexMap = Map with (ByteArray, TopLevelDeclIndex)

// WHERE a top-level constant is filed, and the ONE place a constant's visibility becomes a fact
// about its NAME.
//
// A FILE-PRIVATE constant is filed under a key carrying its declaring file's path; an EXPORTED one
// under its bare name. That single distinction is what makes `file-private-same-name-cross-file`
// work — two files may each declare `let SHARED` with a different value, and each file's reads
// resolve to its OWN — and it is why "reject duplicates" is not merely unnecessary here but
// FORBIDDEN by the spec.
//
// Ported from v1's `topLevelConstStorageName` (Parser.maxon:3221-3226) + `fileScopedTopLevelKey`
// (Project.maxon:189-191). v1's rule has three conditions (a bare name, file-private, immutable);
// shv2's has ONE, and P1.0d.5b is why the third is gone.
//
// ⭐ **THE KEY EXTENDS TO A `var`. THE LABEL IS WHAT DOES NOT.** This comment used to say the
// opposite — "when the `var` rung lands it MUST NOT extend this scoping" — and it was half right
// in a way worth preserving, because the half it saw is real:
//
//   • IDENTITY must be scoped. Two files may each declare a file-private `counter`
//     (`global-file-private-same-name.md`, and `top-level-let.md` states the same rule for a
//     `let`), and each file's reads must resolve to ITS OWN. That is a property of the KEY, it is
//     the same property for both kinds of declaration, and rejecting the second declaration is not
//     an available reading of the spec.
//   • What the old comment correctly saw is that a `var` needs something a `let` never did: a
//     **LABEL**. A constant inlines at every read, so there is no slot and no name in the IR. A
//     var's slot is real, and a label carrying a FILE PATH would put a temp directory into every
//     golden fragment and into the emitted binary.
//
// It concluded that the key therefore could not be scoped. The actual conclusion is that these are
// TWO facts and want two fields: **keep ONE key, and DERIVE the label from it** (`dataLabelFor`) —
// path-free, bare-name-based, disambiguated only where two keys collide on one label.
//
// ⚠ **v1 CONCEDES THE BUG THIS AVOIDS.** Its `not mutable` condition leaves file-private vars
// sharing one bare key, and its own comment admits the reason is empirical, not structural: "In
// practice no two file-private vars in the self-hosted/stdlib tree share a bare name"
// (Parser.maxon:3206-3214). The C# bootstrap has the same defect and it is MEASURED: its resolver
// is file-scoped while its `.data` label is global-by-name, so `global-file-private-same-name.md`
// returns 212 where 118 is correct — `bumpA()` reads featB's slot. Correctness resting on a
// property of the corpus is not correctness. NONE of v1's four bare-key sites is ported.
function topLevelStorageKey(name ByteArray, visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
	if visibility == Visibility.file 'filePrivate'
		return fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: declFilePath)
	end 'filePrivate'
	return name
end 'topLevelStorageKey'

// The key a FILE-PRIVATE declaration is filed under. Also the first probe `declFor` makes, which
// is why it is a function and not an inlined interpolation at the two sites: a reader and a writer
// that spelled this key differently would file a declaration nothing could ever find.
function fileScopedDeclKey(name ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
	return "{declFilePath.toString()}{FileScopedKeySeparator}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
end 'fileScopedDeclKey'

// The prefix every top-level `var`'s `.data` label carries — a NAMESPACE, and today nothing more.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT ROUTE THE SECTION, and an earlier draft of this comment claimed it did.** What
// puts a global in writable `.data` is the RELOCATION KIND: `encodeLeaRegGlobal` (X64Backend) files
// every global's `lea` as `RdataRelocKind.dataSectionRipRelDisp32` unconditionally, and
// `patchRdataRelocationsX86` measures that kind — and only that kind — from `.data`'s RVA. The
// label's TEXT is never consulted on the way. MEASURED: with this constant set to `"zzz_"` the
// suite's globals still land in `.data`, still take their stores, and the program still returns the
// right answer. The claim it "would be classified `rdata` and fault on its first store" was false.
//
// So what it buys is READABILITY — `__data_counter` in a golden fragment says what it is — plus
// v1's convention, which the day a global is registered BY NAME will matter for real.
//
// ⭐ **THIS IS NOW THE ONLY COPY OF THIS STRING.** There was a second — a literal `"__data_"` inside
// `classifyGlobalLabel` (CodeResult.maxon) — and the two could not disagree only because that whole
// family was DEAD: zero callers, ported ahead of a use that never came. It was left in place on the
// prediction that it "plausibly becomes live when the `__slab_`/`__gt_` runtime globals arrive"; P1.0r
// then shipped `__slab_cursor` and `__slab_end` and wired nothing to it, which falsified the
// prediction, so the family was deleted rather than kept as a copy waiting to go stale. A future
// name-based classifier single-sources its prefix HERE.
let DataLabelPrefix = "__data_"

// ⭐ What separates a `.data` label from its collision-breaking ordinal — and the character is
// load-bearing, not cosmetic. **`$` is legal in no Maxon identifier**, so `__data_counter$1` is a
// label only this disambiguator can ever mint: no user-written `var` can collide with one. That is
// exactly what lets `uniqueDataLabel` COUNT instead of SEARCH (below).
let DataLabelDisambiguator = "$"

// How many `.data` labels `assignDataLabels` has minted for each BARE label so far.
//
// A COUNTER, not a set of taken labels — and the difference is a quadratic. The obvious shape is
// "probe `__data_counter`, then `$1`, then `$2`, … until one is free", which is O(k) probes for the
// k-th global of a name and therefore **Θ(n²) for n same-named file-private globals** — precisely
// the shape `global-file-private-same-name.md` is about, so precisely the shape that must not be
// the slow path. The counter answers in O(1) because it does not have to ask whether a candidate is
// free: `$` is unwritable in source (see `DataLabelDisambiguator`), so this map is the ONLY minter
// of suffixed labels and its count IS the next free ordinal. Nothing else can have taken it.
typealias DataLabelCountMap = Map with (ByteArray, MachineWord)

// `name`'s `.data` label, and the bump of `minted` that reserves it. The FIRST global of a bare name
// gets the bare label; the k-th gets `__data_<name>$<k-1>`.
//
// O(1), with no search — see `DataLabelCountMap`. Deterministic because `assignDataLabels` walks the
// arena in declaration order (source-path, then in-file), so WHICH declaration gets the bare name is
// a function of the program and not of a hash iteration order.
function uniqueDataLabel(name ByteArray, minted DataLabelCountMap) returns ByteArray
	let bare = "{DataLabelPrefix}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
	let alreadyMinted = try minted.get(bare) otherwise 0
	minted.upsert(bare, value: alreadyMinted + 1)

	if alreadyMinted == 0 'firstOfThisName'
		return bare
	end 'firstOfThisName'

	return "{String.from(bare)}{DataLabelDisambiguator}{alreadyMinted}".toByteArray()
end 'uniqueDataLabel'

// A top-level constant's state, from `pending` through to its answer. ONE union rather than a value
// table plus a side set of in-progress names, because the two questions the evaluator asks — "what
// is this worth?" and "am I already inside it?" — are the same question asked at different times,
// and a name that could be in one structure but not the other is exactly how a cycle goes undetected.
export union TopLevelConstant
	// Read by the sweep, not yet evaluated. Every decl starts here.
	pending
	// On the current evaluation's DFS stack: a reference that reaches this decl again is a CYCLE.
	evaluating
	// It evaluated. `payload` is the number (a bool's is its 0/1 — TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`), and
	// `tag` says which of the two it is. This is what a use INLINES.
	value(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	// THIS declaration is defective, and `error` is the positioned diagnostic for it. Held rather
	// than reported, because the sweep that found it is deliberately TOLERANT and has no business
	// vetoing a program (see `foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`). The REAL parse raises it, once, when it
	// reaches this line — which is also the only place that knows the diagnostic is about source the
	// user actually wrote rather than a name some other file mentioned.
	failed(error ParseError)
	// It has no value only because a constant it READS has none. Silent by design: a second
	// diagnostic here would name a line whose only fault is mentioning a broken name. v1 does the
	// same, dropping cycle victims so their readers see a plain "Undefined name" rather than a
	// cascade (TypeResolution.maxon:8332-8338).
	poisoned
end 'TopLevelConstant'

// A top-level `let` OR `var`, as the declaration sweep found it: WHAT it is called, WHETHER it is
// mutable, WHERE it was written, and the TOKEN RANGE of its initializer — read, never evaluated on
// the spot.
//
// ⭐ **ONE ARENA FOR BOTH KINDS, AND THAT IS THE RUNG'S CENTRAL CLAIM.** A top-level `var` is not a
// new declaration form; it is this one with `mutable: true` and a home. Its initializer obeys the
// IDENTICAL rule (`static-variables.md`: "Top-level `var` initializers must be constant expressions
// (same rules as `let`)"), so it wants the identical evaluator, the identical memoized DFS, the
// identical cycle detection and the identical forward-reference-by-deferred-evaluation trick. A
// second arena would be a second reading of the same grammar — the duplication this file's header
// says the compiler pays for hardest — and the two would be free to disagree about what `BASE * 2`
// is worth. What a `var` adds is settled in exactly two fields: `mutable` and `dataLabel`.
//
// **THE RANGE, AND NOT A TREE.** v1 lowers each initializer into a flat arena of structural nodes
// (`Project.unresolvedConstExprs`, Project.maxon:3361-3432) because its parse and its resolution are
// separated by whole passes: by the time TypeResolution evaluates a constant, the Parser that read it
// is long gone. shv2 has no such gap — the sweep and the evaluation are two halves of ONE query
// (`Queries.queryProgramSignatures`), and the token memo is live throughout — so the range is
// enough, and a node union would be a THIRD representation of an expression this compiler already
// has two of. The C# bootstrap makes the same call for the same reason: its `ConstantDecl` is a
// `(TokenStart, TokenEnd)` pair (2-Parser.cs:1678) and `EvaluateConstant` rewinds a cursor onto it
// (2-Parser.cs:4960-4964).
//
// `tokens` is the DECLARING file's token array, carried so that a reference FROM ANOTHER FILE can be
// resolved without reaching back into the query layer for it. It is a reference to the very array
// `queryTokens` memoized, never a copy — the token cache retains it for the whole build regardless,
// so carrying it here costs one pointer.
export type TopLevelDecl
	// The key this declaration is filed under — `topLevelStorageKey`'s answer, computed once at
	// construction so that the sweep's write and every later read cannot spell it differently.
	export var storageKey as ByteArray
	// The BARE name, kept for the diagnostics that must print what the user wrote rather than the
	// path-qualified key the compiler files it under. (v1 needs a `displayTopLevelKey` to strip that
	// prefix back off at E2012 time; carrying the bare name costs one pointer and needs no stripping.)
	export var name as ByteArray
	// `var` (mutable, a real `.data` slot) or `let` (immutable, inlined at every read). The one bit
	// that decides which of the two doors a USE goes through — `constantValueOf` for a constant,
	// `globalDeclFor` for a var — and the reason they are separate doors at all.
	export var mutable as bool
	export var declFilePath as FilePath
	export var tokens as TokenArray
	export var initStart as TokenIndex
	export var initEnd as TokenIndex
	// The NAME's position — where a diagnostic about this declaration points.
	export var nameLine as LineNumber
	export var nameColumn as ColumnNumber
	// The evaluator's answer, mutated in place as the DFS reaches and leaves this decl.
	export var outcome as TopLevelConstant
	// ⭐ THE `.data` LABEL — this var's IR IDENTITY, and EMPTY for a `let`, which has no storage.
	//
	// DERIVED FROM THE KEY, not a second spelling of it: `assignDataLabels` walks the arena in
	// declaration order and hands each mutable decl its bare name, disambiguated ONLY where an
	// earlier decl already took that label. So the common program gets `__data_counter` and the
	// pathological one gets `__data_counter` + `__data_counter$1` — and NEITHER carries a file path,
	// which is what keeps a golden fragment free of the temp directory the spec runner compiled in.
	//
	// Empty is not a sentinel here: it is the literal truth that an immutable declaration has no
	// slot, and every reader gates on `mutable` before looking (`dataLabelOf` panics rather than
	// handing back an empty label).
	export var dataLabel as ByteArray

	export static function create(name ByteArray, mutable bool, visibility Visibility, declFilePath FilePath, tokens TokenArray, initStart TokenIndex, initEnd TokenIndex, nameLine LineNumber, nameColumn ColumnNumber) returns TopLevelDecl
		return Self{
			storageKey: topLevelStorageKey(name, visibility: visibility, declFilePath: declFilePath),
			name: name,
			mutable: mutable,
			declFilePath: declFilePath,
			tokens: tokens,
			initStart: initStart,
			initEnd: initEnd,
			nameLine: nameLine,
			nameColumn: nameColumn,
			outcome: TopLevelConstant.pending,
			dataLabel: b""
		}
	end 'create'
end 'TopLevelDecl'

export typealias TopLevelDeclArray = Array with TopLevelDecl

// What the arena knows about a bare name — `CalleeReturn`'s twin, and a union for its reason: "no
// constant of this name is visible here" is an ANSWER (the name is probably a local), not a failure.
union TopLevelDeclLookup
	found(index TopLevelDeclIndex)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelDeclLookup'

// What a constant expression evaluates to: the PAYLOAD, and the TAG that says what the payload
// means. A bool's payload is its 0/1 (TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`), which is why one integer field
// serves both — and why the tag is not optional: a `boolean` 1 and an `integer` 1 are the same
// number and different values.
//
// A `type` (so, one heap object per operand folded), which the per-VALUE rules in TypeRules go to
// real lengths to avoid — and the trade is the other way here. Those run on every operator of every
// expression of every function; this runs once per top-level constant DECLARATION, of which a
// program has a handful. A tagged pair is what the code wants to say, and there is no hot path to
// pay for it.
export type ConstValue
	export var payload as ParsedInt
	export var tag as ValueTypeTag

	export static function create(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ConstValue
		return Self{payload: payload, tag: tag}
	end 'create'
end 'ConstValue'

// The result of asking the evaluator for a constant's value. `failed` is not an error to report — the
// diagnostic is already recorded on the decl that owns it — it is the instruction to STOP.
export union ConstEvalOutcome
	value(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	failed
	notFound
end 'ConstEvalOutcome'

// Why a constant's initializer produced no value. Distinct from `ParseError` because the evaluator
// has one outcome `ParseError` cannot express: "the defect is somewhere else, and is already
// reported".
export union ConstEvalError implements Error
	// THIS initializer is defective; `error` is the positioned diagnostic for it.
	defective(error ParseError)
	// A constant this one READS has already been diagnosed. Carries no diagnostic of its own — see
	// `TopLevelConstant.poisoned`.
	dependencyFailed
end 'ConstEvalError'

// The outcome of a USE-SITE constant lookup, after evaluation has finished. Deliberately NOT
// `ConstEvalOutcome`: a use site cannot trigger an evaluation (they are all done by then) and must
// not be able to, so it is given a type with no `failed` arm to mishandle.
export union TopLevelConstantLookup
	found(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelConstantLookup'

// The outcome of a USE-SITE lookup for a top-level `var` — `TopLevelConstantLookup`'s twin, and
// deliberately a DIFFERENT type carrying DIFFERENT payload. A constant's use needs its VALUE (it
// inlines a number); a var's use needs its LABEL and TYPE (it emits a load against a slot). One
// union carrying both would have an arm every caller had to prove it was not holding.
export union TopLevelGlobalLookup
	found(label ByteArray, tag ValueTypeTag)
	notFound
end 'TopLevelGlobalLookup'

// Which kind of top-level declaration a lookup is asking for. The two are resolved by ONE probe
// (`declFor`) and are never interchangeable at a use site: a constant inlines, a var loads.
//
// An enum, not the bare `mutable bool` the decl carries: at a CALL SITE `wanted: DeclKind.constant`
// says what is being asked for, where `wanted: false` would be a magic value the reader has to
// decode against the field's polarity.
enum DeclKind
	constant
	mutable
end 'DeclKind'

// The `StdType` a global of `tag` occupies its `.data` slot as — which is to say WHICH TAGS MAY BE
// A GLOBAL AT ALL, because the width itself is not this function's to know.
//
// ⚠ **IT NO LONGER CARRIES ITS OWN TABLE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** It used to answer `integer`→i64
// and `boolean`→i1 in its own two arms, which made it a SECOND copy of the tag→`StdType` collapse
// that `LowerMaxonToStd.valueTagToStdType` already is — one fact written down twice, in the
// project's signature failure mode. The two copies had already begun to drift in the only way they
// could: this one had no `float` arm, so `var pi = 3.14` panicked here while the lowering was
// perfectly willing to give it an f64. A global's width and a value's width are THE SAME QUESTION,
// and `data-section-f64-8byte` pins the 8 bytes that answer proves.
//
// What is genuinely local is the REACHABILITY guard below — what a top-level `var` can be typed —
// and that is a different question from how wide the type is. `named` needs an `as` cast (P1.9) and
// a managed global needs P1.2's heap (`Parser.readTopLevelBinding` rejects one before it gets
// here), so they panic rather than defaulting to a width.
//
// `boolean` is ONE byte (`i1`, whose `storageBytes` is 1), which is what
// `static-variables.md`'s `data-section-bool-1byte` pins; `integer` is eight (`i64`); `float` is
// eight (`f64`). All three come from the one table.
function globalStdType(tag ValueTypeTag, name ByteArray) returns StdType
	match tag 'reachable'
		boolean to float then break 'reachable'
		named then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a global's type is inferred from a bare constant initializer, so it is never a ranged alias (that needs an `as` cast, P1.9)")
		exitCode to void then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — the constant evaluator folds integers, bools and floats only")
		// A STRUCT GLOBAL is the case this guard's header was written FOR, arriving one rung early.
		// It is unreachable by the same route the others are — the constant evaluator folds no
		// `T.create(…)`, because a call in a top-level initializer is E2045 — so a struct's tag
		// cannot get this far. Were it to, the honest answer is still not a width: a struct global
		// needs a `.data` slot holding a POINTER to a box allocated before `main`, which is module
		// initialization, and shv2 has none.
		structRef then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a struct global would need its box allocated before `main` runs, which needs module initialization (not built); a call in a top-level initializer is rejected as E2045 long before this")
		// A String global is rejected at parse (`requireStorableGlobal` — only int/bool/float have
		// `.data` storage; a String is a heap/rdata pointer that a `.data` image cannot hold as a
		// constant), so it never reaches here.
		string then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a String global is a pointer a constant `.data` image cannot hold, rejected at parse by requireStorableGlobal")
		// A FUNCTION global is unreachable (P1.5-A1): a global takes no type annotation and a function
		// reference is not a constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never `function` (a bare
		// function name at top level is rejected before this — `var g = double` is E2045, no call in a
		// constant initializer). Were it to reach here, the honest answer is still not a width.
		function then panic("globalStdType: top-level var '{String.from(name)}' is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a function value is not a constant initializer, so a global's inferred type is never a function")
	end 'reachable'

	return valueTagToStdType(tag)
end 'globalStdType'

// What the index knows about a callee — the TWO outcomes the old `MaxonType.unresolved` sentinel
// collapsed into one, plus the third (`void`) that it dropped on the floor.
export union CalleeReturn
	// Some file declares this function, and `returnType` is its declaration UNDISTORTED: `void`
	// means it returns nothing at all (using the call as a value is an error — `Parser.parseCall`),
	// `value(t)` means it returns a `t`. The old code mapped BOTH of these and the case below onto
	// one `unresolved`, which is how `let x = noop()` then `x + 4` compiled.
	declared(returnType MaxonReturnType)
	// NO file declares it. The parser DEFERS — it types the call `unresolved` — rather than
	// reporting, and that is deliberate: the authoritative answer belongs to
	// `SemanticCheck.validateCall`, which reads the registry the REAL parse built (E3004, "call to
	// undefined function"). This token sweep is the TOLERANT half of the pair (it records only the
	// exact `function NAME ( … ) returns TYPE` shape and skips anything else without complaint), so
	// it must never gain a veto: a sweep that missed a declaration the parser accepts would reject
	// a correct program. Two whole-program tables that could each veto a call would also be one
	// fact written down twice — and this one is not the authority.
	undeclared
end 'CalleeReturn'

// What the index knows about a NAMED type — `CalleeReturn`'s twin, and a union for the same
// reason: "no file declares this" is an ANSWER, not a failure, and spelling it as a sentinel range
// (`low: 0`) would make an undeclared name read as UNSIGNED, which is the one thing it must never
// silently do.
export union DeclaredAlias
	declared(alias RangedTypeAlias)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredAlias'

// What the index knows about a FUNCTION typealias (P1.5-A1) — `DeclaredAlias`'s twin, and a union for
// the identical reason: "no file declares a function alias of this name" is an ANSWER, not a failure.
// THE door `Parser.parseTypeReference` asks "is this name a function type?" through — a hit re-tags the
// reference `MaxonType.function`; a miss leaves it a `named` for the ranged/struct/enum registries.
export union DeclaredFunctionAlias
	declared(alias FunctionTypeAlias)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredFunctionAlias'

// What the index knows about a declared STRUCT TYPE — `DeclaredAlias`'s twin, and a union for the
// identical reason. "No file declares a `type` of this name" is an ANSWER: the name is probably a
// ranged alias, or `ExitCode`, or a typo — and each of those is somebody else's question. Handing
// back an empty layout instead would make an undeclared name read as a struct with NO FIELDS, so
// `p.x` would report "no field 'x'" for a type that is not a struct at all.
export union DeclaredStruct
	declared(layout StructLayout)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredStruct'

// What the index knows about a declared ENUM/UNION type — `DeclaredStruct`'s twin, and a union for
// the identical reason. "No file declares an `enum`/`union` of this name" is an ANSWER: the name is
// probably a struct, a ranged alias, or a typo — each somebody else's question. THE door the parser
// asks every enum question through: is this scrutinee an enum? what are its cases and tags?
export union DeclaredEnum
	declared(layout EnumLayout)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredEnum'

// ⭐ **HOW A UNION PAYLOAD FIELD IS STORED AND DROPPED — P1.3 slice 2.** The ONE classifier the
// construct (move-in), the match extract (move-out), the drop routing, and the synthesized
// `__destruct_<U>` cascade all read, so "is field F managed, and if so String or struct?" is decided in
// exactly ONE place. That is the whole defence against this rung's signature bug: a field moved IN but
// not dropped leaks; dropped but not moved in double-frees — and neither can happen when one function
// decides both. It is v1's `unionHasManagedPayload` predicate generalised to also name the KIND.
export union PayloadClass
	// int / bool / exitCode / a ranged-int alias / a payload-free enum (whose value IS its i64 tag).
	// Stored inline; owns nothing; the cascade skips it.
	scalar(storage MaxonType)
	// A String (`isString`) or a struct box — an owned heap pointer. Construct MOVES it in (no incref);
	// a match binding MOVES it out; the cascade drops it (String via `__str_decref`, struct via
	// `__mm_decref`). `storage` is the field's own type, which `maxonTypeToStdType` gives a machine word.
	managed(storage MaxonType, isString bool)
	// A `float` payload — needs the bitcast ops a later rung adds (a scalar-only union is int-tagged).
	floatPayload
	// A boxed-union field/payload (a nested payload-bearing union) — a MANAGED owned heap pointer, exactly
	// like a `managed` struct box: construct MOVES it in (no incref), and the cascade drops it through its
	// OWN `__destruct_<U>` (or the trivial `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only union box). It is kept a case
	// DISTINCT from `managed` so the UNION-payload path — whose store width comes from `payloadStorageOf`
	// and CANNOT carry the `named` tag through `maxonTypeToStdType` — can still reject a nested boxed-union
	// PAYLOAD cleanly (`requirePayloadLowerable`); the STRUCT-field path derives its store width from
	// `fieldStorageType` instead, so it lowers a boxed-union field fine (P1.4b wave 2c). `storage` is the
	// field's `named(<union>)` type — the value tag a struct-field type-check compares, a moved-out load
	// carries, AND the union-payload reject derives its "union U" message text from (at the error site, not
	// eagerly — the message is a cold path, so nothing precomputes a `String` on every classification).
	nestedUnion(storage MaxonType)
	// A name no file declares — `TypeResolution` reports it as E3011; the caller keeps the op
	// well-formed for the instant before the pipeline's error gate throws.
	undeclaredName
end 'PayloadClass'

// Classify a union payload field's storage and ownership. `typeNames` resolves a `named` field's id to
// a NAME; pass the interner the LAYOUT's ids live in — the file-local artifact interner at the
// construct/extract sites, `project.typeNames` at the destructor-synthesis site. All three lookups
// below are keyed by that name, so they are interner-independent once the name is in hand.
export function classifyUnionPayload(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns PayloadClass
	match fieldType 'kind'
		boolean then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		integer then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		exitCode then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		float then return PayloadClass.floatPayload
		// A String / struct reference is a managed heap pointer whose move-in and drop are this rung's
		// work. A payload field of a struct's own name resolves to `structRef` (`Self` and the enclosing
		// type); a reference to ANOTHER struct arrives as `named` and is resolved below.
		string then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: true)
		structRef then return PayloadClass.managed(fieldType, isString: false)
		unresolved then panic("classifyUnionPayload: a payload field is declared `unresolved` — `parseTypeReference` produces only `named`, `structRef` and the three primitive keywords, so no payload declaration can carry one")
		// A FUNCTION-typed payload (P1.5-A1) is a SCALAR: a code pointer is an i64 stored inline, owns no
		// heap, and the cascade skips it (nothing to drop). It stores through `maxonTypeToStdType(function)`
		// → i64, exactly as a scalar does.
		function then return PayloadClass.scalar(fieldType)
		named then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'

	let name = try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("classifyUnionPayload: a payload field's named type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the layout's ids live in")

	// A ranged-int alias is a scalar of its underlying width; a float-underlying alias would be a float
	// payload (none are declarable yet).
	match signatures.aliasOf(name) 'alias'
		declared(alias) then return aliasPayloadClass(alias)
		undeclared then break 'alias'
	end 'alias'

	// A payload-FREE enum's value is an i64 tag — a scalar; a BOXED union is a managed heap pointer
	// (its box carries the field's `named(<union>)` id, which `enumPayloadClass` reads off `fieldType`).
	match signatures.enumOf(name) 'asEnum'
		declared(enumLayout) then return enumPayloadClass(enumLayout, fieldType: fieldType)
		undeclared then break 'asEnum'
	end 'asEnum'

	// A `type` (struct) payload is a managed heap pointer. Its storage type is a `structRef` — NOT the
	// `named` the field was spelled with: `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a `named` (an op's type must be
	// concrete by lowering), and a struct BINDING's field access (`b.mass`) requires the `structRef`
	// tag (`requireStructBase`). An undeclared name is a user error TypeResolution reports as E3011.
	match signatures.structOf(name) 'asStruct'
		declared then return PayloadClass.managed(MaxonType.structRef(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)), isString: false)
		undeclared then return PayloadClass.undeclaredName
	end 'asStruct'
end 'classifyUnionPayload'

// A ranged-int alias payload is a scalar of its underlying width. A float-underlying alias would be a
// float payload, rejected the same way a bare `float` is.
function aliasPayloadClass(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns PayloadClass
	if maxonTypeTag(alias.underlying) == ValueTypeTag.float 'floatAlias'
		return PayloadClass.floatPayload
	end 'floatAlias'
	return PayloadClass.scalar(alias.underlying)
end 'aliasPayloadClass'

// A payload whose type is another enum/union: a payload-FREE enum is a scalar (its value is its i64
// tag, stored inline); a BOXED union is a MANAGED heap pointer carrying the field's own `named(<union>)`
// type (`fieldType`) — the value tag its move-in/move-out/drop all read.
function enumPayloadClass(enumLayout EnumLayout, fieldType MaxonType) returns PayloadClass
	if enumLayout.isBoxed() 'boxed'
		return PayloadClass.nestedUnion(fieldType)
	end 'boxed'
	return PayloadClass.scalar(MaxonType.integer)
end 'enumPayloadClass'

// ⭐ **THE ONE LOOP THREE QUESTIONS SHARE.** Does any case carry a payload that needs a DROP — a managed
// (String/struct) field, or (when `wantStringOnly`) specifically a String field? Keeping it ONE function,
// not one copy per site, is what makes the destructor's dropped set, the construct's moved set, and the
// `usesStrDecref` gate agree on what "managed" means by construction. Callers:
//   • `wantStringOnly: false` — does the union need a `__destruct_<U>` at all / does the construct move
//     its payloads in (`decrefCalleeFor`, `installUnionDestructors`, `noteUnionDestructorUsage`);
//   • `wantStringOnly: true`  — does that destructor transitively call `__str_decref` (the usesStrDecref
//     gate in `noteUnionDestructorUsage`).
export function unionHasManagedField(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, layout EnumLayout, wantStringOnly bool) returns bool
	for ci in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
		if caseHasManagedField(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, layout: layout, ci: ci, wantStringOnly: wantStringOnly) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachCase'
	return false
end 'unionHasManagedField'

// The per-CASE half — the destructor's tag switch emits a drop block only for a case this answers true
// for, so the switch and the union-wide gate above read the exact same classification.
export function caseHasManagedField(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, layout EnumLayout, ci CaseIndex, wantStringOnly bool) returns bool
	let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(ci)
	for fi in 0 upto payloads.count() 'eachField'
		let field = try payloads.get(fi) otherwise panic("caseHasManagedField: payloadsAt count disagrees with get at {fi}")
		let class = classifyUnionPayload(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, fieldType: field.declaredType)
		let needsDrop = payloadClassIsString(class) if wantStringOnly else payloadClassIsManaged(class)
		if needsDrop 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachField'
	return false
end 'caseHasManagedField'

// Is a classified payload a managed (String/struct) one — the single reader of "does this class own a
// heap drop?", so every site testing it (the predicate above, the destructor synthesis, the usage scan)
// agrees.
export function payloadClassIsManaged(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		managed gives true
		nestedUnion gives true
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsManaged'

// Is a classified payload a STRING (drops through `__str_decref`) rather than a struct (`__mm_decref`)?
// The single reader of "which managed drop?", so the destructor synthesis and the usesStrDecref gate route
// the same way.
export function payloadClassIsString(class PayloadClass) returns bool
	return match class 'c'
		managed(_, isString) gives isString
		scalar gives false
		floatPayload gives false
		nestedUnion gives false
		undeclaredName gives false
	end 'c'
end 'payloadClassIsString'

// The CONCRETE storage type a classified payload occupies its slot as — what the `loadIndirect`/
// `storeIndirect` carries. A managed payload's storage is a `structRef`/`string` (an i64 pointer); a
// boxed-union (`nestedUnion`) payload's is the field's `named(<union>)` — the value tag a struct-field
// type-check compares against the value (`requireManagedValueMatches`, tag-only, never lowered here).
// A `float` payload is rejected before any store reaches here. ⚠ A `named` storage does NOT lower
// through `maxonTypeToStdType` (it panics), so the UNION-payload store — whose width comes from
// `payloadStorageOf` — must never receive a `nestedUnion` (`requirePayloadLowerable` rejects it first);
// the STRUCT-field store derives its width from `fieldStorageType` (→ i64), not from here.
export function payloadStorageOf(class PayloadClass) returns MaxonType
	match class 'c'
		scalar(storage) then return storage
		managed(storage, _) then return storage
		nestedUnion(storage) then return storage
		undeclaredName then return MaxonType.integer
		floatPayload then panic("payloadStorageOf: a float payload has no lowerable storage — requirePayloadLowerable rejects it before any store")
	end 'c'
end 'payloadStorageOf'

// The MaxonType a LOADED payload value is tagged with: a managed payload's concrete storage (so a
// struct binding reads as `structRef` — required by `requireStructBase` — and a String as `string`); a
// boxed-union payload's `named(<union>)` (so a moved-out load is recognized as a boxed union by
// `enumMatchFor`/`decrefCalleeFor`); a scalar's DECLARED type (so a ranged alias's low bound stays
// readable by the shift rule, exactly as `emitFieldLoad` keeps a scalar field's alias).
export function payloadValueTypeOf(class PayloadClass, declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType
	match class 'c'
		scalar then return declared
		managed(storage, _) then return storage
		nestedUnion(storage) then return storage
		undeclaredName then return declared
		floatPayload then panic("payloadValueTypeOf: a float payload has no lowerable value type — requirePayloadLowerable rejects it before any load")
	end 'c'
end 'payloadValueTypeOf'

export typealias PayloadClassArray = Array with PayloadClass

// ⭐ **A FUNCTION'S PER-PARAMETER CONSUME FACTS (P1.4a wave 2)** — the ordered `paramNames` (so a call
// site can slot a LABELLED argument `inner: i` to its position without the `FuncSignature`, which is not
// yet built at parse time) and the `bits` (`bits[i]` true iff parameter `i` is CONSUMED — moved into a
// struct field by the function's own body). The sweep computes them from tokens (the only whole-program
// input a pure per-file parse may read), one entry per CONSUMING function, and travels by NAME with no
// interner (a name and a bool are not ids), exactly like `enumTypes`.
export type ParamConsumeInfo
	export let paramNames as ByteArrayArray
	export let bits as BoolArray

	export static function create(paramNames ByteArrayArray, bits BoolArray) returns ParamConsumeInfo
		return Self{paramNames: paramNames, bits: bits}
	end 'create'

	// The declaration index of the parameter named `name`, or `paramNames.count()` when absent — the
	// label→position slotting a call site needs, the `FuncSignature.indexOfParam` shape over the sweep's
	// own name list.
	export function indexOfParam(name ByteArray) returns ParamCount
		for i in 0 upto self.paramNames.count() 'each'
			let paramName = try self.paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("ParamConsumeInfo.indexOfParam: paramNames.get OOB at {i}")
			if paramName.equals(name) 'match'
				return i
			end 'match'
		end 'each'
		return self.paramNames.count()
	end 'indexOfParam'

	export function isConsumed(index ParamCount) returns bool
		if index >= self.bits.count() 'past'
			return false
		end 'past'
		return try self.bits.get(index) otherwise panic("ParamConsumeInfo.isConsumed: bits.get OOB at {index} despite index < count")
	end 'isConsumed'
end 'ParamConsumeInfo'

export type ParamConsumeEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let info as ParamConsumeInfo

	export static function create(name ByteArray, info ParamConsumeInfo) returns ParamConsumeEntry
		return Self{name: name, info: info}
	end 'create'
end 'ParamConsumeEntry'

export typealias ParamConsumeEntryArray = Array with ParamConsumeEntry
export typealias ParamConsumeMap = Map with (ByteArray, ParamConsumeInfo)

// One function's `throws E` clause, captured by the declaration sweep (P1.4b) — the argument- and
// call-site dual of its return type, from the SAME token walk. `recordScannedSignature` fills one per
// THROWING function so a call site can decide whether it needs `try` (E3055/E3057) and what type an
// `(e)` binding recovers. Travels by NAME and carries no ids, exactly like `ParamConsumeEntry`.
export type ThrowsClauseEntry
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let clause as ThrowsClause

	export static function create(name ByteArray, clause ThrowsClause) returns ThrowsClauseEntry
		return Self{name: name, clause: clause}
	end 'create'
end 'ThrowsClauseEntry'

export typealias ThrowsClauseEntryArray = Array with ThrowsClauseEntry
export typealias FuncThrowsMap = Map with (ByteArray, ThrowsClause)

// The answer to "does this callee consume any parameter?" — a union so "no recorded entry" is an ANSWER
// (a borrow-only callee), never confused with an empty-but-present one.
export union CalleeConsume
	consumes(info ParamConsumeInfo)
	borrowsAll
end 'CalleeConsume'

// ⭐ **WHICH RUNTIME ENTRY DROPS A MANAGED FIELD OF DECLARED TYPE `fieldType`** — the ONE router shared by
// the parser's value-keyed `decrefCalleeFor` (a top-level owned value's drop) and the struct/union
// destructor cascade (each managed field's drop), so a field moved IN through one classifier is dropped
// through the matching callee and cannot leak (missed drop) or double-free (wrong drop). It generalises the
// union cascade's `StrDecrefName if isString else MmDecrefName` line, which could not reach a nested managed
// struct/union: a `String` drops via `__str_decref`; a struct or boxed union that itself owns managed heap
// drops via ITS `__destruct_<T>` cascade; a scalar-only struct or scalar-only union box takes the trivial
// `__mm_decref` (its box is all it owns).
//
// `typeNames` resolves a `named`/`structRef` field id to a NAME; pass the interner the field type's ids live
// in (the file artifact interner at a construct/extract site, `project.typeNames` at the synthesis site).
// The field is a precondition MANAGED one — every caller asks `structFieldIsManaged`/`payloadClassIsManaged`
// first, so a scalar reaching here is a classification drift and panics rather than mis-routing.
export function managedFieldDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, fieldType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(fieldType)
	if tagIsText(tag) 'string'
		return StrDecrefName
	end 'string'
	if not (tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.named) 'notManagedRef'
		panic("managedFieldDropCallee: a field of tag '{typeTagName(tag)}' is not a managed heap reference — every caller classifies the field managed first")
	end 'notManagedRef'
	let name = try typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("managedFieldDropCallee: a managed field's type id is not in the interner it was resolved against — pass the interner the field type's ids live in")
	return managedNameDropCallee(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, name: name)
end 'managedFieldDropCallee'

// The drop callee for a managed field NAMED `name` — a declared struct or boxed union. Split from
// `managedFieldDropCallee` so a `structRef` (which is always a struct) and a `named` (struct or union) reach
// the same struct/union lookup once the name is in hand.
function managedNameDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if signatures.containsStruct(name) 'struct'
		if signatures.structHasManagedField(name) 'managedStruct'
			return destructorSymbol(name)
		end 'managedStruct'
		return MmDecrefName
	end 'struct'
	match signatures.enumOf(name) 'union'
		declared(layout) then return unionNameDropCallee(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, layout: layout)
		// A managed field that is neither struct nor enum cannot occur: `structFieldIsManaged` classified it
		// managed, and its only managed non-String forms are a declared struct and a boxed union.
		undeclared then panic("managedNameDropCallee: managed field '{String.from(name)}' is neither a declared struct nor a declared enum/union — the caller classified it managed")
	end 'union'
end 'managedNameDropCallee'

// A boxed union that itself owns a managed payload drops through its own cascade; a scalar-only union box
// owns only its box.
function unionNameDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, layout EnumLayout) returns ByteArray
	if unionHasManagedField(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, layout: layout, wantStringOnly: false) 'managedUnion'
		return destructorSymbol(layout.name)
	end 'managedUnion'
	return MmDecrefName
end 'unionNameDropCallee'

export type ProgramSignatures
	// Function name -> its declared return type. Last-wins on a duplicate name, exactly as the
	// project's own `funcReturnTypes` registry is; the duplicate itself is reported by
	// `commitFuncSignatures` (E3006), which fails the build, so the overwrite is never observed.
	var returnTypes as FuncReturnTypeMap
	// Typealias name -> its declaration, with the RANGE the shift rule needs. It carries no
	// type-name IDS — a ranged alias's underlying type is always the `int` KEYWORD, never a name —
	// so unlike `returnTypes` it needs no interner and no remap: it travels by name and arrives by
	// name.
	var typeAliases as RangedTypeAliasMap
	// FUNCTION typealias name -> its declaration (P1.5-A1). Like `typeAliases` it carries NO type-name
	// ids — a function alias's return type is stored interner-free as a (tag, name-ByteArray) pair (see
	// FunctionTypeAlias) — so it travels by name and arrives by name, needing no interner and no remap.
	// THE door `Parser.parseTypeReference` asks "is this name a function alias?" through, and the one an
	// indirect call recovers the callee's return type from.
	var functionTypeAliases as FunctionTypeAliasMap
	// ⭐ Struct-type name -> its declaration, with the FIELD ORDER the parser needs. THE door `p.x`
	// asks through, and the reason it must be here rather than in `Project`: a field's offset is
	// needed at PARSE time, and `type Point` may be declared BELOW the function that reads a
	// `Point` or in another file entirely. The same argument the header makes for a callee's return
	// type, about a different declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ **UNLIKE `typeAliases`, IT CARRIES IDS** — a field's declared type is a `MaxonType`, and
	// `named(Integer)` is meaningless outside the interner that minted it. So it obeys the header's
	// ID PROBLEM: `foldFile` remaps each file's ids into `typeNames` on the way in, and
	// `fieldTypeOf` re-interns on the way out. It is the second table to need that treatment, and
	// it needs it for exactly `returnTypes`' reason.
	var structTypes as StructLayoutMap
	// ⭐ Enum/union name -> its declaration, with the CASE ORDER and TAGS the parser needs. THE door
	// an enum `match` asks through — is `c` an enum? what tag is `red`? which cases does `low to
	// high` cover? — and here for `structTypes`' reason: the answer is needed at PARSE time and the
	// `enum` may be declared below its use or in another file.
	//
	// ⚠ **LIKE `structTypes`, IT CARRIES IDS** — a case NAME and a tag INT carry none, but a
	// PAYLOAD field's declared type is a `MaxonType`, and `holds(inner BoxA)`'s `named(BoxA)` is
	// meaningless outside the interner that minted it. So a payload-bearing union obeys the header's
	// ID PROBLEM exactly as `structTypes` does: `foldFile` remaps each file's payload ids into
	// `typeNames` on the way in (`remapEnumLayoutPayloadTypes`, the enum twin of
	// `remapStructLayoutFieldTypes`), and the parser re-interns on the way out (`adoptType`, the
	// same door `fieldTypeOf` uses). A payload-FREE enum carries no ids and travels by name alone.
	var enumTypes as EnumLayoutMap
	// The interner every `named` id in `returnTypes` belongs to. PRIVATE, and it has to be: an id
	// may not leave this type. `returnTypeOf` re-interns it into the caller's own table.
	var typeNames as TypeNameInterner
	// THE TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT ARENA — every `let` declared at module scope in any file, in
	// DECLARATION order (source-path order, then in-file order).
	//
	// An ARRAY is the driver and a map is only the index into it, which is the opposite of the two
	// tables above and is deliberate: the E2012 cycle message names its members in the order the DFS
	// met them, so the evaluation order is part of what the compiler PRINTS. Driving from a map would
	// make it depend on hash order — v1 drives from a map and has to sort the names back into
	// declaration order to undo exactly that (`sortTopLevelNamesByPosition`,
	// TypeResolution.maxon:8268-8304). Here there is nothing to sort: the sweep builds the array in
	// the order the message needs.
	var decls as TopLevelDeclArray
	// Storage key -> its index in `decls`. FIRST-WINS on a duplicate key (see `recordDecl`).
	var declIndex as TopLevelDeclIndexMap
	// ⭐ Function name -> its per-parameter CONSUME bits (P1.4a wave 2). `paramIsConsumed(callee, i)` is
	// the door a call site asks "does the callee take ownership of my argument at position i?". Filled by
	// `recordParamConsume` from each file's `scanParamConsumes`; last-wins on a duplicate name (a duplicate
	// function is E3006, reported elsewhere, so the overwrite is never observed), exactly as `returnTypes` is.
	var funcParamConsumes as ParamConsumeMap
	// ⭐ Function name -> its `throws E` clause (P1.4b). `throwsOf(callee)` is the door a call site asks
	// "does this callee throw, and what error type?". Filled by `recordThrows` from each file's scanned
	// signatures; last-wins on a duplicate name (E3006 reported elsewhere), exactly as `returnTypes` is.
	// Only THROWING functions are recorded — a callee with no entry is non-throwing, the safe default that
	// makes recording only the throwing ones correct (the same "absence is the answer" `funcParamConsumes`
	// rests on).
	var funcThrows as FuncThrowsMap
	// The content hash OF THE DECLARATIONS — not of the source they were read from. This is the
	// second half of a parse memo's key (`QueryEngine.parseMemoKey`), and hashing the folded
	// ENTRIES rather than the FILES is the whole point of it:
	//   • edit a function's BODY  ⇒ that file's content hash moves (it re-parses); this hash does
	//     NOT (no other file re-parses). Incrementality is preserved.
	//   • edit a RETURN TYPE      ⇒ this hash moves, and every file re-parses. Which is exactly
	//     right: every call to that function, in any file, may have changed meaning.
	// Keying the parse memo on the composite SOURCE hash instead would be sound and would throw
	// away the first property — one file's edit would re-parse the whole program.
	//
	// ⚠ A TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT PUTS ITS **VALUE** IN HERE, NOT JUST ITS NAME, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING
	// (`mixConstant`). A constant is the one declaration whose value is COPIED INTO its readers — a
	// use inlines it as a literal — so editing `export let MAGIC = 42` to `43` changes what every
	// other file COMPILES TO while leaving those files' own bytes, and so their own content hashes,
	// untouched. Only this hash can carry that. Measured, not assumed: with the warm-rebuild gate's
	// declaration probe pointed at a constant, a 7 → 8 edit re-parses every file in the project; the
	// gate's `invalidation` property is what keeps it that way.
	export var hash as ContentHash

	export static function create() returns ProgramSignatures
		return Self{returnTypes: FuncReturnTypeMap.create(), typeAliases: RangedTypeAliasMap.create(), functionTypeAliases: FunctionTypeAliasMap.create(), structTypes: StructLayoutMap.create(), enumTypes: EnumLayoutMap.create(), typeNames: TypeNameInterner.create(), decls: TopLevelDeclArray.create(), declIndex: TopLevelDeclIndexMap.create(), funcParamConsumes: ParamConsumeMap.create(), funcThrows: FuncThrowsMap.create(), hash: fnvOffsetBasis()}
	end 'create'

	// Fold ONE file's scanned declarations into the index. Called in SOURCE-PATH order, so the
	// index — and its hash — are a deterministic function of the program, not of the order the
	// files happened to be visited in.
	//
	// `fileTypeNames` is the interner the scan interned into (a `Parser`'s own artifact-local
	// table), so its ids are file-local. They are folded in and remapped through exactly the
	// machinery `mergeArtifact` uses for the identical job — `TypeNameInterner.foldInto` +
	// `remapMaxonReturnType`. One mechanism, two callers: a second copy of "reconcile a file's ids
	// into a bigger table" would be free to disagree with this one about what an id means.
	export function foldFile(scanned FuncReturnTypeEntryArray, aliases RangedTypeAliasArray, functionAliases FunctionTypeAliasArray, structs StructLayoutArray, enums EnumLayoutArray, bindings TopLevelDeclArray, paramConsumes ParamConsumeEntryArray, throwsEntries ThrowsClauseEntryArray, fileTypeNames TypeNameInterner)
		for alias in aliases 'eachAlias'
			self.recordAlias(alias)
		end 'eachAlias'

		// Function aliases travel by NAME and carry no interner ids (their return type is stored as a
		// tag + a name ByteArray), so they fold here alongside the ranged aliases, before the interner
		// reconciliation below — nothing about them needs remapping (like `paramConsumes`/`throwsEntries`).
		for functionAlias in functionAliases 'eachFunctionAlias'
			self.recordFunctionTypeAlias(functionAlias)
		end 'eachFunctionAlias'

		// Consume facts travel by NAME and carry no ids, so they fold before the interner reconciliation
		// below — nothing about them needs remapping (like `enumTypes`).
		for entry in paramConsumes 'eachConsume'
			self.recordParamConsume(entry.name, info: entry.info)
		end 'eachConsume'

		// Throws clauses travel by NAME and carry no ids either (the throws type is a bare enum name),
		// so they fold here alongside the consume facts, before the interner reconciliation.
		for entry in throwsEntries 'eachThrows'
			self.recordThrows(entry.name, clause: entry.clause)
		end 'eachThrows'

		// Bindings — `let` and `var` alike — are RECORDED here and EVALUATED later
		// (`evaluateInitializers`), and the gap between the two is the whole reason a forward
		// reference works — see that function.
		for binding in bindings 'eachBinding'
			self.recordDecl(binding)
		end 'eachBinding'

		let remap = self.typeNames.foldInto(fileTypeNames)

		// A declared struct's field types are reconciled IN PLACE, before the record loop below, so
		// there is ONE recording path rather than a mapped and an unmapped one. That is the opposite
		// shape from the signatures below, and the difference is what each fold owns: a
		// `MaxonReturnType` is a union VALUE, so remapping it can only mean constructing a new one
		// (hence the two loops, and the identity fast path that skips an allocation per declared
		// function); a `StructLayout` is a heap `type` this sweep just built and still owns, so its
		// field list is simply rewritten. `remapStructLayoutFieldTypes` is the single mechanism
		// `mergeArtifact` folds the same records with.
		// A declared type's id-bearing member types — a struct's field types and a union/enum's
		// PAYLOAD field types (`walk(dir Direction)`) — are reconciled IN PLACE before the record
		// loops below, so there is ONE recording path rather than a mapped and an unmapped one. A
		// payload-free enum carries no ids, so its loop body rewrites nothing; the record loop still
		// runs unconditionally (the ids are irrelevant to WHETHER a type is declared, only to what its
		// members mean).
		if not remap.isIdentity 'typeMemberIdsMoved'
			for layout in structs 'eachMovedStruct'
				remapStructLayoutFieldTypes(layout, remap: remap)
			end 'eachMovedStruct'
			for enumLayout in enums 'eachMovedEnum'
				remapEnumLayoutPayloadTypes(enumLayout, remap: remap)
			end 'eachMovedEnum'
		end 'typeMemberIdsMoved'

		for layout in structs 'eachStruct'
			self.recordStruct(layout)
		end 'eachStruct'

		for enumLayout in enums 'eachEnum'
			self.recordEnum(enumLayout)
		end 'eachEnum'

		// The remap is loop-INVARIANT, so the identity test is hoisted OUT of the per-declaration
		// loop rather than asked once per function — the same shape `mergeArtifact` uses for the same
		// fold. And the identity case is not merely a shortcut, it avoids an ALLOCATION per declared
		// function in the program: `remapMaxonReturnType` CONSTRUCTS a `MaxonReturnType`, and a Maxon
		// union value is a heap object (every case), so remapping ids that did not move would allocate
		// a fresh copy of the object it was handed, for nothing.
		//
		// ⚠ Written as two loops over one `record`, NOT as `x if remap.isIdentity else remap(x)`. The
		// ternary form LEAKS under the C# bootstrap — its arms mix a BORROWED field read with a
		// freshly-OWNED value, and the conditional does not reconcile the two ownerships (measured:
		// `MM leak: 7 allocation(s) remain` on every build, exit 101). Recorded in OPEN.md.
		if remap.isIdentity 'idsUnchanged'
			for entry in scanned 'eachUnmapped'
				self.record(entry.name, returnType: entry.returnType)
			end 'eachUnmapped'
			return
		end 'idsUnchanged'

		for entry in scanned 'eachRemapped'
			self.record(entry.name, returnType: remapMaxonReturnType(entry.returnType, remap: remap))
		end 'eachRemapped'
	end 'foldFile'

	// Add one declaration to the index AND fold it into the hash. The one write, so the table and the
	// hash that keys every parse memo on it can never disagree about what the index contains.
	function record(name ByteArray, returnType MaxonReturnType)
		self.returnTypes.upsert(name, value: returnType)
		self.hash = self.mixDeclaration(self.hash, name: name, returnType: returnType)
	end 'record'

	// Add one function's consume bits to the index AND fold them into the hash. The one write, for
	// `record`'s reason: the table and the hash that keys every parse memo on it cannot disagree about what
	// the index contains. A function with NO consumed parameter is not recorded (its default answer is "not
	// consumed"), so the common function costs no map entry and no hash movement — the same "absence is the
	// answer" the sweep's over-approximation rests on.
	function recordParamConsume(name ByteArray, info ParamConsumeInfo)
		self.funcParamConsumes.upsert(name, value: info)
		var state = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeParamConsume), bytes: name)
		// The param NAMES ride too: a rename changes which labelled argument a caller slots to a consumed
		// position, so a caller's memo must move when they do (the slotting `indexOfParam` reads them).
		for i in 0 upto info.paramNames.count() 'eachName'
			let paramName = try info.paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("recordParamConsume: paramNames.get OOB at {i} despite i < count")
			let bit = info.isConsumed(i)
			state = mix(mixBytes(state, bytes: paramName), value: ConsumeBitSet if bit else ConsumeBitClear)
		end 'eachName'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordParamConsume'

	// ⭐ **DOES `callee` CONSUME (take ownership of) ITS ARGUMENT AT POSITION `index`?** THE door the
	// callee-side enrollment asks per parameter. A callee with no recorded entry, or an index past its
	// recorded bits, is a BORROW (false) — the safe default, and what makes recording only the consuming
	// functions correct.
	export function paramIsConsumed(callee ByteArray, index ParamCount) returns bool
		return match self.consumeOf(callee) 'c'
			consumes(info) gives info.isConsumed(index)
			borrowsAll gives false
		end 'c'
	end 'paramIsConsumed'

	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE'S CONSUME FACTS, or `borrowsAll`** — the door a CALL SITE asks once, then slots each
	// argument against `info.paramNames` to decide which ones are moved. `borrowsAll` (no recorded entry) is
	// every runtime callee, every borrow-only user function, and the overwhelming common case — so the call
	// site's fast path is a single `match` with nothing to slot.
	export function consumeOf(callee ByteArray) returns CalleeConsume
		let info = try self.funcParamConsumes.get(callee) otherwise return CalleeConsume.borrowsAll
		return CalleeConsume.consumes(info)
	end 'consumeOf'

	// Record one function's `throws E` clause AND fold it into the hash — the one write, for `record`'s
	// reason. A NON-throwing function is not recorded (its default answer is "does not throw"), so the
	// common function costs no map entry and no hash movement, exactly as `recordParamConsume` treats a
	// borrow-only one.
	function recordThrows(name ByteArray, clause ThrowsClause)
		match clause 'k'
			none then break 'k'
			throwing(throwsName) then self.recordThrowingClause(name, clause: clause, throwsName: throwsName)
		end 'k'
	end 'recordThrows'

	function recordThrowingClause(name ByteArray, clause ThrowsClause, throwsName String)
		self.funcThrows.upsert(name, value: clause)
		// The THROWS TYPE NAME rides too: a callee that changes which error it throws changes what type a
		// caller's `(e)` binding recovers, so the caller's memo must move with it.
		self.hash = mixBytes(mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeThrows), bytes: name), bytes: throwsName.toByteArray())
	end 'recordThrowingClause'

	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE'S `throws` CLAUSE, or `none`** — the door a call site asks: does this callee throw,
	// and what error type does it throw? `none` (no recorded entry) is every non-throwing function and the
	// common case. Read by the parser (to type an `(e)` binding) and by SemanticCheck (E3055/E3057).
	export function throwsOf(callee ByteArray) returns ThrowsClause
		return try self.funcThrows.get(callee) otherwise ThrowsClause.none
	end 'throwsOf'

	// Add one declared struct type to the index AND fold it into the hash. The one write, for
	// `record`'s reason: the table and the hash that keys every parse memo on it cannot disagree
	// about what the index contains.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HASH CARRIES THE FIELD LIST, NOT MERELY THE NAME**, and that is `recordAlias`'s
	// argument about ranges applied to a layout. Every reader of `type Point` COPIES its layout into
	// its own code — `p.y` becomes `loadIndirect … offset 8`, and `Point.create` becomes an
	// `__mm_alloc` of 16 — while the reading file's own bytes never move. So reordering Point's
	// fields, renaming one, or adding a third changes what every other file COMPILES TO, and only
	// this hash can carry it. Without it, adding a field would leave every reader inlining the old
	// offsets against a box that is now a different shape: a silent out-of-bounds read.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY FACT A FIELD CARRIES RIDES THIS HASH, AND `mixField` IS THE ONE PLACE THEY DO.** The
	// two P1.1a-wave-2 facts are here for the reason the field list is, one each way:
	//   • a DEFAULT changes CODEGEN — `export var value = 0` → `= 1` moves the `literal` op every
	//     other file's `Self{}` emits, while that file's own bytes never move;
	//   • MUTABILITY changes ACCEPTANCE — `var count` → `let count` must turn every other file's
	//     `c.count = 5` into E2013, and a reader that did not re-parse would go on compiling it.
	//
	// v1 is the cautionary tale, and its bug is live: `StdlibCache.maxon:1494-1506` serializes 6 of
	// `StructField`'s 7 payload fields — `isMutable` survives, `hasDefault` was never written — so
	// every cached stdlib field arrives claiming it has no default. A hand-listed copy of a record's
	// fields is a copy that can be short by one, silently. Keep the list in ONE function.
	function recordStruct(layout StructLayout)
		self.structTypes.upsert(layout.name, value: layout)

		var state = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeStruct), bytes: layout.name)
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			state = self.mixField(state, layout: layout, index: i)
		end 'eachField'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordStruct'

	// Add one declared enum/union to the index AND fold it into the hash — `recordStruct`'s twin.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HASH CARRIES THE CASE LIST AND THE TAGS, NOT MERELY THE NAME**, for `recordStruct`'s
	// reason applied to an enum. Every reader of `enum Color` COPIES its facts into its own code:
	// `Color.red` becomes an i64 `literal` of the tag, and a `match` over it becomes a chain of
	// compares against the tags plus an exhaustiveness check over the case NAMES. So changing a tag
	// (`red = 5`), reordering the cases (which moves a range arm's coverage), renaming one, or adding
	// a case changes what every other file COMPILES TO — while the reading file's own bytes never
	// move. Only this hash can carry it. The `isUnion` bit rides too: `enum` → `union` changes the
	// word a non-exhaustive-match diagnostic prints, and a reader that met that diagnostic must
	// re-parse to reprint it.
	function recordEnum(layout EnumLayout)
		self.enumTypes.upsert(layout.name, value: layout)

		var state = mix(mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeEnum), bytes: layout.name), value: EnumKindUnion if layout.isUnion else EnumKindEnum)
		for i in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			state = self.mixEnumCase(state, layout: layout, index: i)
		end 'eachCase'
		self.hash = state
	end 'recordEnum'

	// Fold one case's NAME and TAG into the enum's hash. Split out so the SIGNED tag (`ParsedInt`)
	// crosses to the UNSIGNED hash domain through a local — `recordAlias`/`mixDefaultPayload` have
	// the same two lines for the same reason (the bootstrap rejects the cast as unneeded, E3010, when
	// it is written inline as a nested `mix` argument).
	function mixEnumCase(state ContentHash, layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex) returns ContentHash
		let named = mixBytes(state, bytes: layout.caseNameAt(index))
		let tagBits = layout.tagOfIndex(index) as ContentHash
		return mix(named, value: tagBits)
	end 'mixEnumCase'

	// Fold EVERYTHING the compiler reads off field `index` into the index's hash. See `recordStruct`
	// for why each fact must ride, and why they must all ride from one place.
	function mixField(state ContentHash, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns ContentHash
		let fieldName = try layout.fieldNames.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.mixField: fieldNames.get OOB at {index}")
		let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.mixField: fieldTypes.get OOB at {index} — the field columns fell out of lockstep")

		let named = self.mixType(mixBytes(state, bytes: fieldName), t: fieldType)
		let withMutability = mix(named, value: FieldShapeMutable if layout.fieldIsMutable(index) else FieldShapeImmutable)
		return self.mixFieldDefault(withMutability, defaultValue: layout.fieldDefault(index))
	end 'mixField'

	// The DEFAULT half of `mixField`. `none` mixes its own shape rather than nothing: a field that
	// gained a default must hash differently from one that never had it, and `= 0` on a field whose
	// payload would otherwise fold to the same 0 is exactly the collision that would hide it.
	function mixFieldDefault(state ContentHash, defaultValue FieldDefault) returns ContentHash
		return match defaultValue 'shape'
			none gives mix(state, value: FieldShapeNoDefault)
			literal(payload) gives self.mixDefaultPayload(state, payload: payload)
		end 'shape'
	end 'mixFieldDefault'

	// The payload half of a defaulted field's hash. Split from `mixFieldDefault` so the payload
	// arrives as a bound PARAMETER: it is SIGNED (`ParsedInt` — and a float default's is a bit
	// pattern that routinely has its top bit set) while the hash domain is unsigned, so it crosses
	// by an explicit cast, and that cast needs a local. `recordAlias` has the same two lines for the
	// same reason: written inline as an argument to a nested `mix`, the bootstrap misreads it as
	// already-hashed and rejects the cast as unneeded (E3010) — a false positive that costs nothing
	// to route around.
	function mixDefaultPayload(state ContentHash, payload ParsedInt) returns ContentHash
		let payloadBits = payload as ContentHash
		return mix(mix(state, value: FieldShapeDefaulted), value: payloadBits)
	end 'mixDefaultPayload'

	// Add one ranged typealias to the index AND fold it into the hash. The hash carries the RANGE
	// and not merely the name, and that is load-bearing: widening `int(0 to 100)` to
	// `int(0 to u64.max)` makes the type UNSIGNED, which changes what every `shr` on it MEANS. Every
	// file must re-parse on that edit, and this is what makes it happen.
	function recordAlias(alias RangedTypeAlias)
		self.typeAliases.upsert(alias.name, value: alias)

		// The bounds are SIGNED (`ParsedInt`) and the hash domain is UNSIGNED, so each crosses by an
		// explicit cast. Bound to locals rather than written inline: as arguments to a nested `mix`
		// the bootstrap misreads them as already-hashed and rejects the casts as unneeded (E3010) —
		// a false positive, and one that costs nothing to route around.
		let low = alias.low as ContentHash
		let high = alias.high as ContentHash

		let named = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeAlias), bytes: alias.name)
		self.hash = mix(mix(named, value: low), value: high)
	end 'recordAlias'

	// Add one FUNCTION typealias to the index AND fold it into the hash — the one write, for
	// `recordAlias`'s reason. The RETURN type rides the hash (its tag, then its name): a caller that
	// binds a function value of this alias and calls it recovers the result type FROM this return type,
	// so a change to it changes what that caller compiles to and its parse memo must move with it.
	function recordFunctionTypeAlias(alias FunctionTypeAlias)
		self.functionTypeAliases.upsert(alias.name, value: alias)
		let named = mixBytes(mix(self.hash, value: DeclShapeFunctionAlias), bytes: alias.name)
		self.hash = mixBytes(mix(named, value: alias.returnTag.rawValue as ContentHash), bytes: alias.returnTypeName)
	end 'recordFunctionTypeAlias'

	// Add one top-level DECLARATION (a `let` or a `var`) to the arena. No value yet:
	// `evaluateInitializers` supplies that once every file has been folded in.
	//
	// FIRST-WINS on a duplicate storage key, matching the bootstrap's `decls.FirstOrDefault(d =>
	// d.Name == name)` (2-Parser.cs:4950). ⚠ A file that declares the same name twice is NOT yet
	// diagnosed — the second declaration is silently unreachable. That is a real gap and it is not
	// this rung's: it needs the positioned duplicate check `commitFuncSignatures` does for functions
	// (E3006), and no spec case covers it. It is NOT the cross-file same-name case, which is legal and
	// which `topLevelStorageKey` handles by giving the two decls different keys.
	function recordDecl(decl TopLevelDecl)
		if self.declIndex.contains(decl.storageKey) 'duplicate'
			return
		end 'duplicate'

		self.declIndex.upsert(decl.storageKey, value: self.decls.count())
		self.decls.push(decl)
	end 'recordDecl'

	// ⭐ EVALUATE EVERY TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT IN THE PROGRAM, and fold each answer into the index's hash.
	// Called once, by `queryProgramSignatures`, AFTER every file's declarations have been folded in.
	//
	// **THAT ORDERING IS THE FEATURE.** A forward reference — `let TOTAL = FIRST + SECOND` written
	// above `let FIRST = 30` — resolves precisely because nothing is evaluated until every declaration
	// is KNOWN. A single forward pass cannot do it, and neither reference compiler tries: v1 defers
	// the whole job to TypeResolution, and the bootstrap pre-scans the entire token stream before it
	// parses a line (`CollectAndEvaluateTopLevelDecls`, 2-Parser.cs:1680-1784).
	//
	// Driving over the ARENA in declaration order is what makes the cycle message deterministic — see
	// the `decls` field.
	export function evaluateInitializers()
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'eachDecl'
			let decl = self.declAt(i)
			match decl.outcome 'state'
				// Already settled by an earlier decl's DFS — it referenced this one.
				value to poisoned then break 'state'
				evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.evaluateInitializers: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is still marked `evaluating` between two top-level walks — every DFS pops what it pushes")
				pending then self.startDecl(i)
			end 'state'
		end 'eachDecl'

		self.assignDataLabels()

		// The hash covers the ANSWERS, folded in arena order once they all exist. It cannot be done
		// inside the loop above: a decl's outcome is not final until every DFS that could reach it has
		// run (a cycle rewrites its members' outcomes from the inside).
		for j in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'eachOutcome'
			let decl = self.declAt(j)
			self.hash = self.mixConstant(self.hash, decl: decl)
		end 'eachOutcome'
	end 'evaluateInitializers'

	// ⭐ **GIVE EVERY TOP-LEVEL `var` ITS `.data` LABEL — the second half of the key/label split**
	// (see `topLevelStorageKey`). The KEY answers "which declaration is this?" and is scoped by file;
	// the LABEL answers "which slot does the IR name?" and must not be, because a label carrying a
	// path would land in every golden fragment and in the emitted binary.
	//
	// **BARE NAME, SUFFIXED ONLY ON COLLISION.** Two file-private `counter`s have DIFFERENT keys and
	// would want the SAME label; the second one to be reached takes `__data_counter$1`. So:
	//   • the ordinary program — every global uniquely named — gets stable, readable, path-free
	//     labels, and its goldens never mention a temp directory;
	//   • the pathological one is CORRECT rather than aliased, which is exactly what the bootstrap
	//     is not (`global-file-private-same-name.md`: it returns 212, aliasing `bumpA` onto featB's
	//     slot, where 118 is correct).
	//
	// Walked in ARENA ORDER, which is source-path order then in-file order — so which of two colliding
	// decls gets the bare name is a deterministic function of the program, not of a hash iteration.
	// That is the same property `decls` is an array for.
	function assignDataLabels()
		var minted = DataLabelCountMap.create()
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'each'
			var decl = self.declAt(i)
			if not decl.mutable 'constantHasNoSlot'
				continue
			end 'constantHasNoSlot'
			decl.dataLabel = uniqueDataLabel(decl.name, minted: minted)
		end 'each'
	end 'assignDataLabels'

	// Open a fresh DFS at arena index `index`. The stack is per-ROOT rather than shared across the
	// whole loop, which costs one empty array per unevaluated root and buys the invariant the panic
	// above checks: a walk that ends leaves nothing marked `evaluating`.
	function startDecl(index TopLevelDeclIndex)
		var stack = TopLevelDeclIndexArray.create()
		_ = self.evaluateDecl(index, stack: stack)
	end 'startDecl'

	// Evaluate the constant at arena index `index` — MEMOIZED, and with CYCLE DETECTION.
	//
	// The answer is recorded ON THE DECL and only summarized in the return: a failure's diagnostic
	// belongs to the declaration that caused it, and is raised (once) by the real parse when it
	// reaches that line. Callers get `failed` — an instruction to stop, not an error to report.
	function evaluateDecl(index TopLevelDeclIndex, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		var decl = self.declAt(index)

		match decl.outcome 'state'
			value(payload, tag) then return ConstEvalOutcome.value(payload, tag: tag)
			failed to poisoned then return ConstEvalOutcome.failed
			// Reached a decl already on this walk's stack: the reference chain has closed on itself.
			evaluating then return self.recordCycle(index, stack: stack)
			pending then break 'state'
		end 'state'

		decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.evaluating
		stack.push(index)
		let outcome = self.walkConstInitializer(decl, stack: stack)
		_ = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.evaluateDecl: stack.pop failed for '{String.from(decl.name)}' — every push is matched here")

		return outcome
	end 'evaluateDecl'

	// Walk one initializer's tokens and settle its decl's outcome.
	//
	// A transient `Parser` is what walks them, and that is the point: the evaluator needs a token
	// cursor, integer-literal decoding, `u16.max` bound resolution, character-literal decoding, and
	// the operator precedence table — every one of which a `Parser` already has and single-sources.
	// Re-spelling any of them here would be a second reading of the grammar, which is the duplication
	// this compiler pays for hardest (see TypeRules' header). The parser it builds emits NO ops: a
	// constant's initializer produces a NUMBER, not code.
	//
	// It is created per DECL, not per file, and per decl is per-constant-in-the-program — this runs
	// once per declaration, never per reference (the memo above sees to that). Programs with no
	// top-level constant build no parser here at all.
	function walkConstInitializer(decl TopLevelDecl, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		var parser = Parser.create(decl.tokens, filePath: decl.declFilePath, namespace: "", signatures: self)
		let value = try parser.evalConstInitializer(decl, stack: stack) otherwise (err) 'noValue'
			return self.recordFailure(decl, error: err)
		end 'noValue'

		decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.value(value.payload, tag: value.tag)
		return ConstEvalOutcome.value(value.payload, tag: value.tag)
	end 'walkConstInitializer'

	// Settle a decl that produced no value: it either OWNS the defect or merely inherited one.
	function recordFailure(decl TopLevelDecl, error ConstEvalError) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		match error 'kind'
			defective(parseError) then decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.failed(parseError)
			dependencyFailed then self.poisonIfUndecided(decl)
		end 'kind'

		return ConstEvalOutcome.failed
	end 'recordFailure'

	// Mark a decl as a casualty of someone else's defect — UNLESS a verdict was already handed to it.
	// `recordCycle` settles every member of a cycle from the inside, and those decls then unwind
	// through here carrying `dependencyFailed`; overwriting would blank the one diagnostic the cycle
	// produced.
	function poisonIfUndecided(decl TopLevelDecl)
		match decl.outcome 'state'
			// `recordCycle` already ruled on this decl.
			failed to poisoned then break 'state'
			evaluating then decl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.poisoned
			value then panic("ProgramSignatures.poisonIfUndecided: '{String.from(decl.name)}' holds a value AND failed to evaluate — an initializer produces one or the other")
			pending then panic("ProgramSignatures.poisonIfUndecided: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is `pending` while unwinding its own evaluation — `evaluateDecl` marks it `evaluating` before the walk")
		end 'state'
	end 'poisonIfUndecided'

	// **THE CYCLE.** `index` is already on `stack`, so the arc from its first appearance to the top is
	// a closed reference chain in which no member can ever be evaluated. Settle all of them here,
	// from the inside, and give exactly ONE the diagnostic.
	//
	// WHICH ONE: the LAST-DECLARED member. `stack` holds arena indices and the arena is in declaration
	// order, so that is simply the largest index in the arc — no source positions to compare. The
	// bootstrap picks the same declaration (`decls.Last(d => evaluating.Contains(d.Name))`,
	// 2-Parser.cs:4954) and anchors on its NAME token, which is what `specs/top-level-let.md`'s
	// `circular-dependency-error` gates byte-for-byte (`3:5` — the `B` of `let B = A + 1`). Every
	// other member is POISONED, so the program reports one E2012 and not one per member.
	//
	// ⚠ DIVERGENCE FROM THE BOOTSTRAP, and it is deliberate. It names the whole in-progress set
	// (`string.Join(", ", evaluating)`), which for `X -> A -> B -> A` prints "X, A, B" — but `X` is
	// not circular, it merely LEADS to a cycle, and a message that says it is is simply wrong. shv2
	// names the ARC (`A, B`). The two agree on every cycle whose root is a member — which is every
	// cycle the corpus has, including the spec's — and disagree only where the bootstrap is
	// misreporting.
	function recordCycle(index TopLevelDeclIndex, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		let arcStart = self.stackPositionOf(index, stack: stack)

		var names = ""
		var owner = index
		for i in arcStart upto stack.count() 'eachMember'
			let member = try stack.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordCycle: stack.get OOB at {i}")
			if i > arcStart 'separator'
				names = "{names}, "
			end 'separator'
			let memberDecl = self.declAt(member)
			names = "{names}{String.from(memberDecl.name)}"
			if member > owner 'laterDeclaration'
				owner = member
			end 'laterDeclaration'
		end 'eachMember'

		var ownerDecl = self.declAt(owner)
		ownerDecl.outcome = TopLevelConstant.failed(ParseError.circularConstants(names, line: ownerDecl.nameLine, column: ownerDecl.nameColumn))

		for j in arcStart upto stack.count() 'eachVictim'
			let member = try stack.get(j) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.recordCycle: stack.get OOB at {j}")
			if member == owner 'ownsTheDiagnostic'
				continue
			end 'ownsTheDiagnostic'
			var victim = self.declAt(member)
			victim.outcome = TopLevelConstant.poisoned
		end 'eachVictim'

		return ConstEvalOutcome.failed
	end 'recordCycle'

	// Where `index` sits on the DFS stack. Its presence is a precondition — `recordCycle` is reached
	// only from the `evaluating` arm, which means this decl is on the current walk — so a miss is an
	// invariant break and not a case to handle.
	function stackPositionOf(index TopLevelDeclIndex, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns TopLevelDeclIndex
		for i in 0 upto stack.count() 'each'
			let member = try stack.get(i) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.stackPositionOf: stack.get OOB at {i}")
			if member == index 'found'
				return i
			end 'found'
		end 'each'

		let target = self.declAt(index)
		panic("ProgramSignatures.stackPositionOf: '{String.from(target.name)}' is marked `evaluating` but is not on the DFS stack — the mark and the stack fell out of lockstep")
	end 'stackPositionOf'

	// Resolve `name` from `readerFilePath` and evaluate what it denotes — the door `Parser`'s
	// constant evaluator asks a NAME REFERENCE through, and the recursion that makes a forward
	// reference (and a cross-file one) work.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `var` IS INVISIBLE HERE, AND THE SPEC PINS IT.** `var b = a + 1` where `a` is itself a
	// top-level `var` is `E2004: Undefined constant 'a'` in the reference compiler — MEASURED, not
	// inferred. And it must be: a constant initializer is folded at COMPILE TIME into bytes in the
	// `.data` image, while a var's value is whatever the program last STORED there. Reading one from
	// the other would bake a snapshot of a mutable location into the image and call it a constant.
	// So `mutableIsInvisible` filters the lookup, and the reader gets the same `notFound` it would
	// get for a name nobody declared — which is exactly the diagnostic the reference produces.
	export function evaluateNamed(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstEvalOutcome
		match self.constantDeclFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'lookup'
			found(index) then return self.evaluateDecl(index, stack: stack)
			notFound then return ConstEvalOutcome.notFound
		end 'lookup'
	end 'evaluateNamed'

	// The value `name` denotes when read from `readerFilePath` — the door a USE SITE asks through
	// (`Parser.topLevelConstantReference`), after every constant has been evaluated.
	//
	// A constant that FAILED reads as `notFound`, so its reader reports a plain undefined-name error
	// rather than inlining a value that does not exist. v1 does exactly this, and says why: it drops
	// the cycle's victims from its registry "so readers get a plain 'Undefined name'"
	// (TypeResolution.maxon:8332-8338). The declaration's own diagnostic has already failed the build,
	// so nothing is lost by the reader being vague about it.
	//
	// A `var` reads as `notFound` here too — not because it is undefined, but because it is not a
	// CONSTANT and this door only answers for constants. Its reader (`Parser.parseVariableReference`)
	// asks `globalDeclFor` next, and emits a LOAD.
	export function constantValueOf(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns TopLevelConstantLookup
		match self.constantDeclFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'lookup'
			found(index) then return self.resolvedValueOf(index)
			notFound then return TopLevelConstantLookup.notFound
		end 'lookup'
	end 'constantValueOf'

	// ⭐ **THE `var` DOOR** — what `name` denotes as a top-level MUTABLE global, read from
	// `readerFilePath`. The twin of `constantValueOf`, and the reason the two are separate: a
	// constant's use INLINES a number and a var's use LOADS from a slot, so a caller that could not
	// tell them apart would have to guess which code to emit.
	//
	// It resolves through the IDENTICAL scoped-then-bare probe (`declFor`), which is the whole point
	// of extending the storage key to vars: this is what makes featA's `counter` and featB's
	// `counter` two slots instead of one.
	export function globalDeclFor(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns TopLevelGlobalLookup
		match self.declFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, wanted: DeclKind.mutable) 'lookup'
			found(index) then return self.resolvedGlobalOf(index)
			notFound then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.notFound
		end 'lookup'
	end 'globalDeclFor'

	// The `.data` label and TYPE of the global at `index` — everything a load or a store needs to
	// name it. A var whose initializer failed reads as `notFound` for `resolvedValueOf`'s reason: the
	// declaration's own diagnostic has already failed the build, and a slot with no value is a slot
	// with no bytes to emit.
	function resolvedGlobalOf(index TopLevelDeclIndex) returns TopLevelGlobalLookup
		let decl = self.declAt(index)
		match decl.outcome 'state'
			value(_, tag) then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.found(self.dataLabelOf(index), tag: tag)
			failed to poisoned then return TopLevelGlobalLookup.notFound
			pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.resolvedGlobalOf: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated at a use site — `evaluateInitializers` runs to completion before any file is parsed")
		end 'state'
	end 'resolvedGlobalOf'

	// The `.data` label of the MUTABLE decl at `index`. Mutability is a precondition, not a case: a
	// constant has no slot, and every caller reached this through a `mutable`-filtered lookup.
	function dataLabelOf(index TopLevelDeclIndex) returns ByteArray
		let decl = self.declAt(index)
		if not decl.mutable 'constant'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.dataLabelOf: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is an immutable top-level constant and has no .data slot — a constant inlines at every read")
		end 'constant'
		if decl.dataLabel.count() == 0 'unlabelled'
			panic("ProgramSignatures.dataLabelOf: top-level var '{String.from(decl.name)}' has no .data label — `assignDataLabels` labels every mutable decl in the arena, and it runs inside `evaluateInitializers`")
		end 'unlabelled'
		return decl.dataLabel
	end 'dataLabelOf'

	// EVERY top-level `var` in the program, in ARENA (declaration) order, with the label and value
	// its `.data` slot carries — the door the backend's data-section layout asks through.
	//
	// It is asked ONCE per compile (`Compiler.declaredGlobals`), never per file: it is O(globals),
	// and a whole-program query under a per-file one is asked once per FILE, which is how a linear
	// walk becomes quadratic (see the ARCHITECTURE's warning on this exact query).
	//
	// A var whose initializer FAILED contributes no slot: the build has already failed on its
	// diagnostic, and there is no value to lay down.
	export function declaredGlobals() returns DataSectionEntryArray
		var entries = DataSectionEntryArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto self.decls.count() 'each'
			let decl = self.declAt(i)
			if not decl.mutable 'constantHasNoSlot'
				continue
			end 'constantHasNoSlot'
			match decl.outcome 'state'
				value(payload, tag) then entries.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(self.dataLabelOf(i)), valueType: globalStdType(tag, name: decl.name), value: payload))
				failed to poisoned then continue
				pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.declaredGlobals: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated — `evaluateInitializers` runs to completion inside `queryProgramSignatures`")
			end 'state'
		end 'each'
		return entries
	end 'declaredGlobals'

	// What the DECLARATION the real parse just read came to — the door `Parser.raiseConstantFailure`
	// asks through, keyed on the decl's own storage key rather than on a bare name (a declaration
	// resolves to ITSELF, never through the scoped-then-bare probe a USE goes through).
	//
	// **THE DRIFT GUARD LIVES HERE.** The sweep decides what is top level by counting block depth over
	// tokens (`Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`); the real parse decides it by actually parsing.
	// Those are two readings of one question, and a miss means they disagreed — after which the
	// constant is absent from the index, every use of it reports "undefined variable", and a correct
	// program is rejected for a reason that names nothing the user wrote. It panics instead, naming
	// the drift, in the discipline `mergeArtifact`'s span-lockstep assert follows.
	export function declaredOutcome(decl TopLevelDecl) returns TopLevelConstant
		let index = try self.declIndex.get(decl.storageKey) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.declaredOutcome: the declaration sweep never recorded top-level constant '{String.from(decl.name)}' of {decl.declFilePath.toString()}, which the parser is dispatching at top level — the sweep's block-depth gate and the parser's structure disagree about what is top level")
		let declared = self.declAt(index)
		return declared.outcome
	end 'declaredOutcome'

	function resolvedValueOf(index TopLevelDeclIndex) returns TopLevelConstantLookup
		let decl = self.declAt(index)
		match decl.outcome 'state'
			value(payload, tag) then return TopLevelConstantLookup.found(payload, tag: tag)
			failed to poisoned then return TopLevelConstantLookup.notFound
			pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.resolvedValueOf: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated at a use site — `evaluateInitializers` runs to completion before any file is parsed")
		end 'state'
	end 'resolvedValueOf'

	// **THE RESOLUTION RULE for a bare name that might be a top-level declaration** — and the reason
	// two files may each declare `let SHARED`, or `var counter`, with a different value.
	//
	// SCOPED FIRST, BARE SECOND. A file-private declaration is filed under a key carrying its declaring
	// file's path, so this file's own `SHARED` is found before anyone's exported one of that name.
	// Ported from v1's `lookupTopLevelConst` (TypeResolution.maxon:2857-2868), which orders the two
	// probes identically and for the identical reason.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE PROBE FOR BOTH KINDS, FILTERED BY `wanted`.** The scoped-then-bare order is the
	// resolution RULE, and it is the same rule for a `let` and a `var` — so it is written once. What
	// differs is only which kind the caller can use, and a caller asking for the wrong kind gets
	// `notFound`, which is the truth: no CONSTANT of that name is visible here, even though a var is.
	// A second copy of this walk for vars would be free to drift on the probe order, and the failure
	// mode of that drift is silent — featA's `counter` resolving to featB's slot.
	function declFor(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath, wanted DeclKind) returns TopLevelDeclLookup
		if let scoped = try self.declIndex.get(fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: readerFilePath)) 'filePrivate'
			return self.lookupIfKind(scoped, wanted: wanted)
		end 'filePrivate'

		// The BARE key holds only EXPORTED declarations (`topLevelStorageKey`), so a hit here is
		// visible BY CONSTRUCTION and there is no second visibility test to get wrong. v1 needs one
		// (`isVisibleFrom`) because it files module- and global-visible constants under the bare key
		// and must tell them apart; shv2 has a single exported visibility until namespaces land, and
		// gains that test on the day it gains the distinction.
		if let exported = try self.declIndex.get(name) 'exported'
			return self.lookupIfKind(exported, wanted: wanted)
		end 'exported'

		return TopLevelDeclLookup.notFound
	end 'declFor'

	// `index`, but only if the decl there is the KIND the caller can use.
	//
	// ⚠ IT DOES NOT FALL THROUGH TO THE NEXT PROBE. A file-private `var counter` SHADOWS an exported
	// `let counter` for a constant reader too — the reader gets `notFound`, not the exported constant.
	// That is the same shadowing every scope in the language has (a local `let` shadows a top-level
	// one — see `Parser.parseVariableReference`), and the alternative would be worse than wrong: a
	// constant initializer would silently reach PAST this file's own declaration of the name to
	// somebody else's, and inline a number the author never wrote.
	function lookupIfKind(index TopLevelDeclIndex, wanted DeclKind) returns TopLevelDeclLookup
		let decl = self.declAt(index)
		let isWanted = match wanted 'k'
			constant gives not decl.mutable
			mutable gives decl.mutable
		end 'k'
		return TopLevelDeclLookup.found(index) if isWanted else TopLevelDeclLookup.notFound
	end 'lookupIfKind'

	// `declFor` restricted to CONSTANTS — the probe both constant doors share, named once so the two
	// cannot drift on which kind they meant.
	function constantDeclFor(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns TopLevelDeclLookup
		return self.declFor(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, wanted: DeclKind.constant)
	end 'constantDeclFor'

	function declAt(index TopLevelDeclIndex) returns TopLevelDecl
		return try self.decls.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.declAt: decls.get OOB at {index} — every index handed out is one this arena minted")
	end 'declAt'

	// Mix one top-level constant's ANSWER into the running hash: the key it is filed under, then its
	// VALUE.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE VALUE, AND NOT MERELY THE NAME.** This is the half a READER's parse memo depends on, and
	// the easy half to leave out. A use INLINES the constant — it becomes a `literal` op carrying the
	// number — so a file that merely reads `MAGIC` compiles differently when `MAGIC` changes, while
	// its OWN bytes, and therefore its own content hash, do not move at all. Without this, editing
	// `export let MAGIC = 42` to `43` would re-parse the declaring file and hand every reader a memo
	// with 42 still baked into it. (`QueryEngine.parseMemoKey` is the other half — see the `hash`
	// field.)
	function mixConstant(state ContentHash, decl TopLevelDecl) returns ContentHash
		let keyed = mixBytes(self.mixMutability(state, mutable: decl.mutable), bytes: decl.storageKey)
		match decl.outcome 'o'
			value(payload, tag) then return self.mixDeclAnswer(keyed, decl: decl, payload: payload, tag: tag)
			// A constant with no value bakes no number into a reader, so every failure hashes alike —
			// but it must still hash differently from every VALUE, or `let X = compute()` becoming
			// `let X = 0` would leave a reader's memo valid. The failure's DETAILS are deliberately not
			// mixed: the build fails either way, and no reader's code depends on which diagnostic it
			// failed with.
			failed to poisoned then return mix(keyed, value: ConstShapeNoValue)
			pending to evaluating then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixConstant: '{String.from(decl.name)}' is unevaluated — `evaluateInitializers` folds the hash only after every walk has finished")
		end 'o'
	end 'mixConstant'

	// ⚠ **MUTABILITY RIDES THE HASH, and it is the half a `var` adds.** `let X = 5` and `var X = 5`
	// carry the same key and the same value and compile to COMPLETELY different code in every reader:
	// a constant becomes a `literal` op, a var becomes `globalAddr` + `loadIndirect`. A reader's own
	// bytes do not move when `let` becomes `var`, so — exactly as for the constant's VALUE — only this
	// hash can carry it, and without it every reader would keep a memo that inlines a number the
	// declaration no longer promises. (`ProgramSignatures.hash`'s field comment is the whole argument;
	// this is the second fact it applies to.)
	function mixMutability(state ContentHash, mutable bool) returns ContentHash
		return mix(state, value: DeclShapeMutable if mutable else DeclShapeConstant)
	end 'mixMutability'

	// ⭐⭐ **MIX WHAT A READER COPIES — AND A `var` DOES NOT COPY ITS VALUE.**
	//
	// This is the one place a `let` and a `var` genuinely diverge, and getting it backwards costs
	// incrementality rather than correctness, which is exactly why it is worth being exact about.
	//
	//   • A `let`'s VALUE is COPIED INTO every reader — a use becomes a `literal` op carrying the
	//     number — so `let X = 7` → `8` changes what other files COMPILE TO while their own bytes
	//     never move. Only this hash can carry that. (`verify-warm-rebuild` property 3(c).)
	//
	//   • A `var`'s value is copied into NOTHING. A reader emits `globalAddr __data_X` +
	//     `loadIndirect` and that is byte-identical whether the initializer said 7 or 8 — the number
	//     reaches only the `.data` IMAGE, which is rebuilt from the arena whenever this query misses.
	//     Verified, not assumed: `globalDeclFor` hands a use site `(label, tag)` and no payload
	//     (`TopLevelGlobalLookup`), and `emitGlobalLoad` deliberately does NOT `recordConst` — a
	//     mutable location's value is not a constant the folder may use.
	//
	// So a var mixes its TAG and its LABEL and stops there:
	//   - the TAG because it decides the load's WIDTH (`i1` moves one byte, `i64` eight), so
	//     `var flag = false` → `var flag = 0` really does change every reader's code;
	//   - the LABEL because it IS the reader's IR identity — the slot the emitted `globalAddr` names.
	//
	// ⚠ Mixing the PAYLOAD too would be SOUND and WRONG: it would re-parse every file in the program
	// whenever any global's initial value moved, which is precisely the "sound, and useless"
	// failure `verify-warm-rebuild`'s property (b) exists to reject — the composite-source-hash key
	// wearing a different hat. `globalValueEditIsLocal` is the probe that keeps this honest.
	function mixDeclAnswer(state ContentHash, decl TopLevelDecl, payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ContentHash
		if not decl.mutable 'constantInlinesItsValue'
			return self.mixConstantValue(state, payload: payload, tag: tag)
		end 'constantInlinesItsValue'

		return mixBytes(self.mixValueTag(mix(state, value: ConstShapeValue), tag: tag), bytes: decl.dataLabel)
	end 'mixDeclAnswer'

	// The TAG rides the hash beside the payload: a `boolean` 1 and an `integer` 1 are the same number
	// and different values, and they compile to different code (an i1 versus an i64).
	//
	// Exhaustive over `ValueTypeTag`, and the four unreachable arms panic rather than hashing: the
	// evaluator produces `integer` and `boolean` and nothing else (its literals are ints, chars and
	// bools; `foldIntCompare` yields a bool; `binOpResultTag` yields one of the two given two of the
	// two). Hashing them "just in case" would be inventing a key for a value that cannot exist, and
	// the day one can, this is where it must be decided rather than defaulted.
	function mixConstantValue(state ContentHash, payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ContentHash
		return mix(self.mixValueTag(mix(state, value: ConstShapeValue), tag: tag), value: payload as ContentHash)
	end 'mixConstantValue'

	// Mix a top-level binding's TAG. Shared by both arms of `mixDeclAnswer`, so a `let` and a `var`
	// cannot come to disagree about what distinguishes a bool from an int — the difference between
	// them is which EXTRA facts ride along (a constant's payload; a var's label), never this one.
	//
	// Exhaustive over `ValueTypeTag`, and the unreachable arms panic rather than hashing: the
	// evaluator produces `integer`, `boolean` and `float` and nothing else (its literals are ints,
	// chars, bools and floats; `foldIntCompare` yields a bool; `binOpResultTag` yields one of them
	// given two of them). Hashing the rest "just in case" would be inventing a key for a value that
	// cannot exist, and the day one can, this is where it must be decided rather than defaulted.
	//
	// P1.0d.4 takes the invitation the old `float to named` arm left ("a float needs P1.0d.4"):
	// `let PI = 3.14` and `var ratio = 2.5` are constants now, so a float tag is REACHABLE and gets
	// the one thing a content hash owes it — a distinct key. It reuses `TypeShapeFloat`, the shape
	// `mixType` already gives a float from the `MaxonType` side: the two functions hash one lattice
	// from two directions, and a float that hashed differently depending on which door it came
	// through is a cache that answers a stale parse for a changed file.
	function mixValueTag(state ContentHash, tag ValueTypeTag) returns ContentHash
		match tag 'k'
			integer then return mix(state, value: TypeShapeInteger)
			boolean then return mix(state, value: TypeShapeBoolean)
			float then return mix(state, value: TypeShapeFloat)
			// `named` stays out: a constant's initializer is a bare expression and shv2 infers
			// `int`/`bool`/`float` from it, never an alias — that needs an `as` cast (P1.9).
			named then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a top-level binding is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a constant's type is inferred from a bare initializer, so it is never a ranged alias (that needs an `as` cast, P1.9)")
			exitCode to void then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a top-level binding is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — the evaluator folds integers, bools and floats only")
			// A struct is unreachable here for `globalStdType`'s reason and one more: the only way to
			// build one is `T.create(…)`, and a CALL in a top-level initializer is E2045 in every
			// compiler in the tree. It stays out of the hash rather than being given a key "just in
			// case" — inventing one for a value that cannot exist is what `mixValueTag` has refused to
			// do since it was written, and the day a struct constant is legal the question is which of
			// its FIELDS a reader copies, which is a decision, not a default.
			structRef then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a top-level binding is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a struct constant would have to be folded from a `T.create(…)` call, and a call in a constant initializer is E2045")
			// A String top-level binding never reaches here: a String `var` is rejected by
			// `requireStorableGlobal`, and a String `let` initializer (a string literal) is not a
			// foldable constant, so the const evaluator rejects it upstream.
			string then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a top-level binding is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a String global is rejected at parse (requireStorableGlobal), and a string-literal constant is not foldable")
			// A FUNCTION top-level binding never reaches here (P1.5-A1): a function reference is not a
			// constant initializer, so the const evaluator never folds one to a `function`-tagged value.
			function then panic("ProgramSignatures.mixValueTag: a top-level binding is tagged '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a function value is not a constant initializer, so it is never folded to a top-level constant")
		end 'k'
	end 'mixValueTag'

	// The ranged typealias `name` denotes, declared in ANY file. THE door the shift rule asks
	// through — see `Parser.valueIsUnsigned`.
	export function aliasOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredAlias
		let alias = try self.typeAliases.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		return DeclaredAlias.declared(alias)
	end 'aliasOf'

	// ⭐ **IS `name` A FUNCTION TYPEALIAS, AND WHAT DOES IT RETURN?** (P1.5-A1). THE door
	// `Parser.parseTypeReference` re-tags a `named` reference `MaxonType.function` through, and the one an
	// indirect call recovers the callee's return type from. `undeclared` is the common answer (most names
	// are not function aliases), spelled as a union arm rather than a sentinel for `DeclaredAlias`'s reason.
	// It carries no interner ids (the return type is a tag + a name ByteArray), so it needs no `into`.
	export function functionAliasOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredFunctionAlias
		let alias = try self.functionTypeAliases.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredFunctionAlias.undeclared
		return DeclaredFunctionAlias.declared(alias)
	end 'functionAliasOf'

	// Is `name` a declared FUNCTION typealias — the `bool` half of `functionAliasOf`, and a HOT-PATH
	// allocation guard, exactly like `containsStruct`/`containsEnum` are for their `*Of` twins. Asked of
	// EVERY `named` type REFERENCE (`parseTypeReference`), field READ (`fieldTypeOf`) and call RESULT
	// (`resolveFunctionAlias`) — most of them ranged int aliases, none of them function aliases — so it
	// must not pay `functionAliasOf`'s `DeclaredFunctionAlias` box (a Maxon union value is a heap object
	// EVERY case, `undeclared` included) just to throw it away. A `Map.contains` allocates nothing. Only
	// the cold indirect-call return-type recovery, which needs the alias itself, calls `functionAliasOf`.
	export function isFunctionAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.functionTypeAliases.contains(name)
	end 'isFunctionAlias'

	// The struct type `name` denotes, declared in ANY file — `aliasOf`'s twin, and the door the
	// PARSER asks every struct question through: is this name a `type`? how big is its box? which
	// slot is `x`?
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LAYOUT IT HANDS BACK CARRIES THIS INDEX'S IDS, so a caller may read its NAMES, its
	// COUNT and its OFFSETS but never its field TYPES.** Those are `MaxonType`s minted against
	// `self.typeNames`, which is private and stays private (the header's ID PROBLEM). `fieldTypeOf`
	// is the one door a field's type comes out of, and it re-interns on the way. The split is not
	// a nicety: `indexOfField`/`offsetOfField`/`sizeBytes` are pure arithmetic over the name column
	// and need no interner at all, so making every caller adopt a whole layout to ask "which slot?"
	// would allocate a copy per field access to answer a question about integers.
	export function structOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredStruct
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredStruct.undeclared
		return DeclaredStruct.declared(layout)
	end 'structOf'

	// Is `name` a declared `type` — the `bool` half of `structOf`, and a HOT-PATH allocation guard, the
	// twin of `containsEnum`. `structOf` returns a `DeclaredStruct`, a Maxon union VALUE that boxes on
	// its `undeclared` case as much as any other, so a caller that only needs "is it a struct?" for the
	// COMMON no answer (`Parser.resolveNamedStruct` asks it of every `named` parameter/return type,
	// most of them ranged int aliases) must not pay that box just to throw it away. A `Map.contains`
	// allocates nothing.
	export function containsStruct(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.structTypes.contains(name)
	end 'containsStruct'

	// The enum/union `name` denotes, declared in ANY file — `structOf`'s twin, and the door the
	// PARSER asks every enum question through: is this scrutinee an enum? what tag is a case? which
	// cases does a range arm cover? The layout it hands back carries plain NAMES and INTS (no ids),
	// so every one of those questions is answered with no interner at all.
	export function enumOf(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredEnum
		let layout = try self.enumTypes.get(name) otherwise return DeclaredEnum.undeclared
		return DeclaredEnum.declared(layout)
	end 'enumOf'

	// Is `name` a declared enum/union — the `bool` half of `enumOf`, and a HOT-PATH allocation guard.
	// `enumOf` returns a `DeclaredEnum`, a Maxon union VALUE that allocates a heap box on its
	// `undeclared` case as much as any other, so a caller that only needs "is it an enum?" for the
	// COMMON no answer (`parseDottedPrimary` asks it of every `Point.create(…)` static call) must not
	// pay `enumOf`'s box just to throw it away. A `Map.contains` allocates nothing.
	export function containsEnum(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.enumTypes.contains(name)
	end 'containsEnum'

	// The runtime entry that drops an owned box of `layout` — `__destruct_<U>` for a managed-payload
	// union (its own tag-conditional cascade), the trivial `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only one. THE
	// door `decrefCalleeFor` routes a boxed union's scope-exit drop through.
	//
	// ⚠ It resolves the layout's payload types against `self.typeNames` — the interner they were
	// minted against (a signatures layout's payload ids live here, remapped in by
	// `remapEnumLayoutPayloadTypes`), NOT the caller's. The interner is NOT the caller's to choose:
	// `decrefCalleeFor` used to pass its own artifact interner, which misread a managed payload
	// whenever a ranged alias perturbed the interning order — answering `__mm_decref` for a
	// managed-payload union (a LEAK) or a wrong `__destruct_<T>` (a wild-free). Keeping the
	// resolution here, over this index's own interner, is the fix (OPEN #52).
	//
	// Precondition: `layout` came from `self.enumOf`, so its payload ids live in `self.typeNames`.
	export function unionBoxDropCallee(layout EnumLayout) returns ByteArray
		return unionNameDropCallee(self, typeNames: self.typeNames, layout: layout)
	end 'unionBoxDropCallee'

	// The DECLARED type of field `index` of struct `structName`, ADOPTED into `into` — the calling
	// file's own artifact interner. `returnTypeOf`'s exact shape and exact reason: a `named` type
	// crosses by NAME and is re-interned on arrival, so the id the parser stores is one its own
	// file's merge will resolve.
	//
	// The struct and the index are preconditions, not cases: every caller reached here through a
	// `structOf` that answered `declared`, and `index` is one `indexOfField` handed out.
	export function fieldTypeOf(structName ByteArray, index FieldIndex, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf: no `type {String.from(structName)}` is declared — every caller asks `structOf` first")
		let declared = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf: field {index} is out of range for `type {String.from(structName)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()} — every index handed out came from `indexOfField`")

		// A FUNCTION-typed field (`export var op as UnaryOp`) arrives as a `named` — the SWEEP that
		// recorded this layout ran before the function alias was known — so it is re-tagged `function`
		// HERE, over this index's own function-alias registry (P1.5-A1). One place, so every reader (a
		// field READ, a `Self{op: op}` construction check, the store-width query) sees a function VALUE
		// and not an int. Re-interned into `into` by NAME exactly as `adoptType`'s `named` arm is.
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) == ValueTypeTag.named 'maybeFunctionAlias'
			let name = self.nameOfId(typeNameIdOf(declared))
			if self.isFunctionAlias(name) 'functionAlias'
				return MaxonType.function(into.intern(name))
			end 'functionAlias'
		end 'maybeFunctionAlias'

		return self.adoptType(declared, into: into)
	end 'fieldTypeOf'

	// Does field `index` of struct `structName` own DROPPABLE HEAP — a String, a struct, or a
	// payload-bearing (boxed) union? Drives the struct's `__destruct_<T>` drop CASCADE: every field this
	// answers `true` for is freed (through its own destructor / decref) when the struct box is torn down.
	// P1.4b wave 2c folded the boxed-union field into that cascade — earlier waves refused such a field
	// because the drop did not yet reach it. It is answered ENTIRELY INSIDE this index — over `self.typeNames`, the
	// interner the layout's types were minted against — so it neither re-interns the field type (no
	// `adoptType`, unlike `fieldTypeOf`) nor boxes a `DeclaredEnum`/`DeclaredStruct` to decide struct- or
	// enum-hood: the `contains` guards answer allocation-free, which matters because a scalar field (the
	// overwhelming majority) must reach the `false` return without allocating. A ranged int alias and a
	// payload-free enum are scalars; a `float`/`int`/`bool` is a scalar.
	//
	// The struct and index are preconditions, not cases — every caller reached here through a `structOf`
	// that answered `declared`, and `index` is one `indexOfField` handed out.
	export function structFieldIsManaged(structName ByteArray, index FieldIndex) returns bool
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsManaged: no `type {String.from(structName)}` is declared — every caller asks `structOf` first")
		let fieldType = try layout.fieldTypes.get(index) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsManaged: field {index} is out of range for `type {String.from(structName)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()} — every index handed out came from `indexOfField`")

		let tag = maxonTypeTag(fieldType)
		if tagIsText(tag) or tagIsStructRef(tag) 'stringOrStruct'
			return true
		end 'stringOrStruct'
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'

		let name = try self.typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsManaged: a `named` field type's id is not in this index's interner — the layout's types were minted against exactly this table")
		if self.structTypes.contains(name) 'namedStruct'
			return true
		end 'namedStruct'
		if not self.enumTypes.contains(name) 'notEnum'
			return false
		end 'notEnum'

		let enumLayout = try self.enumTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structFieldIsManaged: enumTypes.contains({String.from(name)}) is true but get failed")
		return enumLayout.isBoxed()
	end 'structFieldIsManaged'

	// Does struct `structName` own droppable heap in ANY field — the union-side `unionHasManagedField`
	// generalised to a struct, and the gate that decides whether the struct needs a `__destruct_<Struct>`
	// cascade at all (a scalar-only struct's drop is the trivial `__mm_decref`). Answered over the index's
	// own interner, allocation-free per scalar field, exactly as `structFieldIsManaged` is — it is the loop
	// around that per-field predicate, kept ONE function so the destructor synthesis, its usage gate and the
	// caller-side drop routing agree on what "managed" means by construction.
	//
	// The struct is a precondition, not a case: every caller reached here through a `structOf` that answered
	// `declared`.
	export function structHasManagedField(structName ByteArray) returns bool
		let layout = try self.structTypes.get(structName) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures.structHasManagedField: no `type {String.from(structName)}` is declared — every caller asks `structOf` first")
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			if self.structFieldIsManaged(structName, index: i) 'managed'
				return true
			end 'managed'
		end 'eachField'
		return false
	end 'structHasManagedField'

	// The declared return type of `callee`, ADOPTED into `into` — the calling file's own artifact
	// interner. THE one door out of this index (see the header): a `named` type crosses by NAME and
	// is re-interned on arrival, so the id the parser stores is one its own file's merge will
	// resolve.
	export function returnTypeOf(callee ByteArray, into TypeNameInterner) returns CalleeReturn
		let declared = try self.returnTypes.get(callee) otherwise return CalleeReturn.undeclared
		return CalleeReturn.declared(self.adoptReturnType(declared, into: into))
	end 'returnTypeOf'

	function adoptReturnType(rt MaxonReturnType, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonReturnType
		return match rt 'r'
			void gives rt
			value(inner) gives self.adoptValueReturn(rt, inner: inner, into: into)
		end 'r'
	end 'adoptReturnType'

	// Only the ID-BEARING arms are rebuilt. Every other return type is already interner-free, and
	// the STORED union is handed straight back — which is what keeps the per-call path from
	// allocating a fresh `MaxonReturnType` for every `returns bool` in the program (a Maxon union
	// value is a heap object, every case — see MaxonDialect's ValueTypeTag header).
	//
	// ⚠ It cannot simply delegate to `adoptType` and re-wrap unconditionally: that would CONSTRUCT a
	// `MaxonReturnType.value(...)` for every callee in the program, id-bearing or not, which is the
	// allocation the `rt` pass-through exists to avoid. So the id-free arms answer with `rt` here,
	// and `adoptType` answers with `t` there — two functions, one rule, each returning the union it
	// was actually handed.
	//
	// ⚠ The two id-bearing arms are written out, NOT collapsed to a `named to structRef` range:
	// `exitCode` and `unresolved` sit BETWEEN them in `MaxonType`, so that range would silently
	// swallow both and re-wrap them into a freshly allocated union — the band-append invariant,
	// costing an allocation rather than a wrong answer this time.
	function adoptValueReturn(rt MaxonReturnType, inner MaxonType, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonReturnType
		return match inner 'r'
			named gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
			structRef gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
			boolean to float gives rt
			exitCode to unresolved gives rt
			// A `String` return type carries no interned name (it is a builtin, like a primitive), so
			// crossing files needs no re-interning — it passes through unchanged.
			string gives rt
			// A FUNCTION return type carries its governing name id (P1.5-A1), so it re-interns like `named`.
			function gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.adoptType(inner, into: into))
		end 'r'
	end 'adoptValueReturn'

	// Re-intern the name an id-bearing `MaxonType` carries into `into`, so the type is
	// indistinguishable from one the calling file had parsed itself. THE one place this index's ids
	// cross out of it (see the header's ID PROBLEM); its callers — `fieldTypeOf`, `adoptValueReturn`
	// (for `returnTypeOf`), and the parser's union-payload sites — re-intern a member/return/payload
	// type by NAME, and none re-derives it. It re-interns by NAME, never leaking a raw id, so the
	// returned type's id belongs to `into` and the "an id may not leave this type" invariant holds.
	//
	// Exported for the union-payload sites: unlike a struct field or a return type, a union payload's
	// `declaredType` is already in the caller's hand (it fetched the layout and walked `payloadsAt`),
	// so it needs this bare crossing rather than a name+index fetch door like `fieldTypeOf`. Routing
	// those sites through here is what stopped a signatures-interner payload id being classified /
	// minted against the parser's own interner (OPEN #52).
	export function adoptType(t MaxonType, into TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonType
		return match t 'r'
			named(id) gives MaxonType.named(into.intern(self.nameOfId(id)))
			structRef(id) gives MaxonType.structRef(into.intern(self.nameOfId(id)))
			boolean to float gives t
			exitCode to unresolved gives t
			// A `String` carries no name id, so like a primitive it passes through unchanged.
			string gives t
			// A FUNCTION value carries its governing name id (P1.5-A1), re-interned by NAME like `named`.
			function(id) gives MaxonType.function(into.intern(self.nameOfId(id)))
		end 'r'
	end 'adoptType'

	// Mix one declaration into the running hash: the function's NAME, then its return type's SHAPE,
	// then — for a named type — that TYPE's name. Names, never ids: an id depends on the order the
	// index happened to intern in, so hashing one would let a new declaration in an unrelated file
	// shift every later id and invalidate every parse memo in the project for nothing.
	function mixDeclaration(state ContentHash, name ByteArray, returnType MaxonReturnType) returns ContentHash
		return self.mixReturnType(mixBytes(state, bytes: name), returnType: returnType)
	end 'mixDeclaration'

	function mixReturnType(state ContentHash, returnType MaxonReturnType) returns ContentHash
		return match returnType 'r'
			void gives mix(state, value: ReturnShapeVoid)
			value(inner) gives self.mixType(mix(state, value: ReturnShapeValue), t: inner)
		end 'r'
	end 'mixReturnType'

	function mixType(state ContentHash, t MaxonType) returns ContentHash
		return match t 'r'
			boolean gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeBoolean)
			integer gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeInteger)
			float gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeFloat)
			named(id) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: TypeShapeNamed), bytes: self.nameOfId(id))
			exitCode gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeExitCode)
			// `Parser.parseTypeReference` produces only `named` and the three primitive keywords, so
			// a SCANNED signature can never carry `unresolved` — TypeResolution is what mints those,
			// and it runs long after this. The arm exists because the match is exhaustive, and it
			// hashes rather than panicking so that a future producer of `unresolved` signatures is a
			// cache-key question, not a crash.
			unresolved gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeUnresolved)
			// A struct reference, which a scanned signature very much CAN carry: `static function
			// create(…) returns Self` inside `type Point` is recorded as `structRef(Point)` by
			// `Parser.parseTypeReference`, so every declared factory in the program hashes through
			// here. Its own shape (see `TypeShapeStructRef`) and then its NAME — never its id, for
			// `mixDeclaration`'s reason.
			structRef(id) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: TypeShapeStructRef), bytes: self.nameOfId(id))
			// A `String` (P1.2 wave A) — its own shape, and no name to mix (a builtin carries none). A
			// signature declaring `returns String` hashes distinctly from every other return type.
			string gives mix(state, value: TypeShapeString)
			// A FUNCTION value (P1.5-A1) — its own shape, then its governing NAME (never its id, for
			// `mixDeclaration`'s reason).
			function(id) gives mixBytes(mix(state, value: TypeShapeFunction), bytes: self.nameOfId(id))
		end 'r'
	end 'mixType'

	function nameOfId(id TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
		return try self.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("ProgramSignatures: type-name id {id} is not in this index's own interner — every id it stores was minted by foldFile's fold into that table")
	end 'nameOfId'
end 'ProgramSignatures'
